1
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:46,606
-Yes, sweetheart?
-There's a girl wants to see you.

2
00:01:46,675 --> 00:01:49,316
Her name's Wonderly.

3
00:01:49,332 --> 00:01:50,359
-Customer?
-I guess so.

4
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:53,645
You'll want to see her, anyway.
She's a knockout.

5
00:01:53,719 --> 00:01:56,561
Shoo her in, Effie, darling. Shoo her in.

6
00:01:59,793 --> 00:02:02,136
EFFIE:
Will you come in, Miss Wonderly?

7
00:02:02,496 --> 00:02:03,921
Thank you.

8
00:02:08,249 --> 00:02:10,841
-Won't you sit down, Miss Wonderly?
-Thank you.

9
00:02:10,918 --> 00:02:13,811
I inquired at the hotel for the name
of a private detective.

10
00:02:13,898 --> 00:02:15,076
They mentioned yours.

11
00:02:15,143 --> 00:02:17,979
Suppose you tell me about it
from the very beginning.

12
00:02:18,051 --> 00:02:19,733
-I'm from New York.
-Uh-huh.

13
00:02:20,512 --> 00:02:22,409
I'm trying to find my sister.

14
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:24,668
I have reason to believe that she's here...

15
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:27,886
...with a man by the name of Thursby.
Floyd Thursby.

16
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:30,909
I don't know where she met him.

17
00:02:30,992 --> 00:02:33,849
We've never been as close
as sisters ought to be.

18
00:02:33,933 --> 00:02:35,723
If we had, perhaps Corinne would have
told me

19
00:02:35,823 --> 00:02:37,478
she was planning on running away.

20
00:02:37,557 --> 00:02:38,727
Mother and Father are in Honolulu.

21
00:02:38,835 --> 00:02:41,020
I've got to find her before they get back.

22
00:02:41,098 --> 00:02:42,350
-They're coming home the first.

23
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:44,027
-You've had word of your sister?

24
00:02:44,109 --> 00:02:46,401
A letter from her about two weeks ago.

25
00:02:46,478 --> 00:02:48,861
It said nothing
except that she was all right.

26
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:51,136
I sent her a telegram,
begging her to come home.

27
00:02:51,211 --> 00:02:54,132
I sent it to General Delivery.
That was the address she gave.

28
00:02:54,215 --> 00:02:57,790
I waited a week and no answer came,
so I decided to come out here myself.

29
00:02:57,869 --> 00:03:00,785
I wrote her that I was coming.
I shouldn't have done that, should I?

30
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:03,231
It's not always easy to know what to do.

31
00:03:03,307 --> 00:03:04,694
You haven't found her?

32
00:03:04,765 --> 00:03:08,861
No. I told her in my letter that I'd be at
the St. Mark and for her to meet me there.

33
00:03:08,939 --> 00:03:12,797
I waited three whole days. She didn't come,
didn't even send a message.

34
00:03:13,246 --> 00:03:15,104
It was horrible, waiting.

35
00:03:15,785 --> 00:03:17,496
I sent her another letter.

36
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:19,901
Yesterday afternoon,
I went to the post office.

37
00:03:19,975 --> 00:03:23,280
Corinne didn't call for her mail,
but Floyd Thursby did.

38
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:25,738
He wouldn't tell me where Corinne was.

39
00:03:25,857 --> 00:03:27,840
He said she didn't want to see me.

40
00:03:27,921 --> 00:03:29,444
I can't believe that.

41
00:03:29,527 --> 00:03:31,943
He promised to bring her to the hotel,
if she'd come.

42
00:03:32,023 --> 00:03:36,348
He said he knew she wouldn't. He promised
to come himself if she didn't. He--

43
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:38,257
Oh, excuse me.

44
00:03:38,398 --> 00:03:41,055
Oh, it's all right, Miles. Come in.

45
00:03:41,136 --> 00:03:43,825
Miss Wonderly, my partner, Miles Archer.

46
00:03:43,996 --> 00:03:45,880
Miss Wonderly's sister ran away...

47
00:03:45,965 --> 00:03:47,426
...with a fellow named Floyd Thursby.

48
00:03:47,501 --> 00:03:49,267
They're here in San Francisco.

49
00:03:49,341 --> 00:03:51,668
Miss Wonderly has seen Thursby,
has a date to meet him.

50
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:54,466
Maybe he'll bring the sister with him.
Chances are he won't.

51
00:03:54,547 --> 00:03:58,433
Miss Wonderly wants us to find the sister,
get her away from him and back home.

52
00:03:58,510 --> 00:03:59,915
-Right?
-Yes.

53
00:04:01,608 --> 00:04:04,849
Now, it's simply a matter of having a man
at the hotel this evening...

54
00:04:04,922 --> 00:04:07,345
...to shadow him when he leads us
to your sister.

55
00:04:07,425 --> 00:04:10,682
If, after we've found her,
she still doesn't want to leave him....

56
00:04:10,758 --> 00:04:13,316
-Well, we have ways of managing that.
-Yeah.

57
00:04:13,398 --> 00:04:14,582
But you must be careful.

58
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:17,143
I'm deathly afraid of him,
of what he might do.

59
00:04:17,223 --> 00:04:19,992
She's so young. His bringing her here is
a serious--

60
00:04:20,070 --> 00:04:22,324
Mightn't he do something to her?

61
00:04:22,396 --> 00:04:24,781
Leave that to us.
We'll know how to handle him.

62
00:04:24,901 --> 00:04:27,228
But I want you to know
he's a dangerous man.

63
00:04:27,299 --> 00:04:29,787
I honestly don't think
he'd stop at anything.

64
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:33,466
I don't think he'd hesitate to kill Corinne
if he thought it would save him.

65
00:04:33,547 --> 00:04:35,932
Could he cover up by marrying her?

66
00:04:36,012 --> 00:04:38,534
He has a wife and three children
in England.

67
00:04:38,618 --> 00:04:42,210
They usually do, though not always
in England. What's he look like?

68
00:04:42,286 --> 00:04:45,109
He has dark hair
and thick, bushy eyebrows.

69
00:04:45,681 --> 00:04:47,411
He talks in a loud, blustery manner.

70
00:04:47,489 --> 00:04:49,751
He gives the impression of being violent.

71
00:04:49,818 --> 00:04:53,590
He was wearing a light gray suit and
a gray hat when I saw him this morning.

72
00:04:53,659 --> 00:04:56,685
-What does he do for a living?
-I haven't the faintest idea.

73
00:04:56,768 --> 00:04:59,252
-What time's he coming to see you?
-After 8:00.

74
00:04:59,334 --> 00:05:02,250
All right, Miss Wonderly.
We'll have a man there.

75
00:05:02,329 --> 00:05:04,357
I'll look after it myself.

76
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,295
Oh, thank you.

77
00:05:08,639 --> 00:05:09,972
Oh, yes.

78
00:05:20,883 --> 00:05:22,582
Will that be enough?

79
00:05:23,651 --> 00:05:25,544
-Thank you.
-Not at all.

80
00:05:25,992 --> 00:05:28,644
It'll help some if you meet Thursby
in the lobby.

81
00:05:28,719 --> 00:05:30,446
-I will.
-You don't have to look for me.

82
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:32,947
-I'll see you all right.
-Thank you.

83
00:05:40,237 --> 00:05:42,421
-Thank you so much.
-Goodbye.

84
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:48,731
They're right enough.

85
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:51,265
They have brothers in her bag.

86
00:05:51,509 --> 00:05:54,207
-What do you think of her?
-Oh, she's sweet.

87
00:05:54,878 --> 00:05:58,002
Maybe you saw her first, Sam,
but I spoke first.

88
00:05:58,917 --> 00:06:00,507
You've got brains.

89
00:06:01,816 --> 00:06:03,211
Yes, you have.

90
00:06:30,681 --> 00:06:31,866
SPADE:
Hello?

91
00:06:33,578 --> 00:06:35,067
Yeah. Speaking.

92
00:06:36,255 --> 00:06:37,949
Miles Archer dead?

93
00:06:39,143 --> 00:06:40,310
Where?

94
00:06:41,918 --> 00:06:43,584
Bush and Stockton?

95
00:06:44,591 --> 00:06:45,677
Yeah.

96
00:06:46,123 --> 00:06:47,602
In 15 minutes.

97
00:06:48,593 --> 00:06:49,783
Thanks.

98
00:07:19,149 --> 00:07:20,636
Hello, Effie?

99
00:07:20,817 --> 00:07:22,008
It's me.

100
00:07:22,786 --> 00:07:24,510
Now, listen, precious.

101
00:07:24,588 --> 00:07:26,313
Miles has been shot.
[EFFIE SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

102
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:28,453
Yeah. Dead.

103
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:31,588
Now, don't get excited.

104
00:07:32,196 --> 00:07:33,250
Yeah.

105
00:07:34,972 --> 00:07:38,296
Now, you'll have to break the news to Iva.
I'd fry first.

106
00:07:38,799 --> 00:07:40,787
And keep her away from me.

107
00:07:42,143 --> 00:07:43,797
That's a good girl.

108
00:07:45,374 --> 00:07:47,327
Now get right over there.

109
00:07:48,371 --> 00:07:49,899
You're an angel.

110
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:52,100
Bye.

111
00:08:04,753 --> 00:08:08,054
-What do you want here?
-I'm Sam Spade. Tom Polhaus phoned.

112
00:08:08,130 --> 00:08:11,084
Oh, I didn't know you at first.
They're back there.

113
00:08:34,311 --> 00:08:37,765
POLHAUS: Hello, Sam. I figured you'd want
to see it before we took him away.

114
00:08:37,848 --> 00:08:39,338
-Well, thanks, Tom. What happened?

115
00:08:39,416 --> 00:08:41,782
-Got him right through the pump with this.

116
00:08:41,853 --> 00:08:43,219
It's a Webley.

117
00:08:43,689 --> 00:08:45,385
English, ain't it?

118
00:08:45,792 --> 00:08:49,145
Yeah. A Webley-Fosbery .45 automatic,
eight shot. They don't make them anymore.

119
00:08:49,228 --> 00:08:51,651
-How many gone out of it?
-Just one.

120
00:08:51,898 --> 00:08:53,592
SPADE:
Let's see. Shot up here, huh?

121
00:08:53,665 --> 00:08:55,919
Standing like you are,
with his back to the fence.

122
00:08:55,998 --> 00:08:58,162
The man who shot him stood here.

123
00:08:58,370 --> 00:09:00,389
He went over, taking the top of the fence.

124
00:09:00,473 --> 00:09:02,590
Went on down the hill
and got caught on that rock.

125
00:09:02,664 --> 00:09:04,500
That's it. The blast burnt his coat.

126
00:09:04,574 --> 00:09:06,802
-Who found him?
-Man on the beat.

127
00:09:07,212 --> 00:09:10,993
-Anybody hear the shot?
-Somebody must've. We just got here.

128
00:09:11,181 --> 00:09:13,567
You want to go down
and have a look at him?

129
00:09:13,648 --> 00:09:15,845
No, you've seen everything I could.

130
00:09:15,919 --> 00:09:18,179
His gun was tucked away.
Hadn't been fired.

131
00:09:18,251 --> 00:09:19,685
His overcoat was buttoned.

132
00:09:19,789 --> 00:09:23,515
I found a $100 bill in his vest pocket
and 30-some bucks in his pants.

133
00:09:23,593 --> 00:09:25,349
Was he working, Sam?

134
00:09:26,458 --> 00:09:27,580
Well?

135
00:09:27,660 --> 00:09:30,357
-Yeah, he was tailing a guy named Thursby.
-What for?

136
00:09:31,159 --> 00:09:32,214
What for?

137
00:09:32,294 --> 00:09:35,529
He wanted to find out where he lived.
Don't crowd me, Tom.

138
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:38,722
I'm going on down and break the news
to Miles' wife.

139
00:09:38,799 --> 00:09:41,534
It's tough, him getting it like that,
ain't it?

140
00:09:41,606 --> 00:09:43,490
Miles had his faults, like any of us...

141
00:09:43,571 --> 00:09:46,771
...but I guess he must've had
some good points too, huh?

142
00:09:47,072 --> 00:09:48,404
I guess so.

143
00:09:51,048 --> 00:09:52,910
Miss Wonderly, please.

144
00:09:54,307 --> 00:09:56,203
Uh? Checked out? What time?

145
00:09:58,946 --> 00:10:00,746
Any forwarding address?

146
00:10:01,383 --> 00:10:02,542
Thanks.

147
00:10:37,319 --> 00:10:39,906
SPADE: Oh, hello, Tom.
Hello, lieutenant. Come in.

148
00:10:47,524 --> 00:10:48,756
Sit down.

149
00:10:53,067 --> 00:10:54,291
Sit down.

150
00:10:59,009 --> 00:11:00,165
Drink?

151
00:11:02,942 --> 00:11:05,631
Did you break the news
to Miles' wife, Sam?

152
00:11:05,937 --> 00:11:07,273
-Uh-huh.
-How'd she take it?

153
00:11:07,341 --> 00:11:09,705
-I don't know anything about women.
-Since when?

154
00:11:09,774 --> 00:11:11,266
-What kind of a gun do you carry?

155
00:11:11,350 --> 00:11:15,382
-None. I don't like them.
-Course, there are some at the office.

156
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:16,377
-You don't have one here?

157
00:11:16,451 --> 00:11:18,548
-You sure about that?
-Look around.
Turn the dump upside down.

158
00:11:18,622 --> 00:11:20,641
I won't
squawk, if you got a search warrant.

159
00:11:20,723 --> 00:11:21,708
-We don't wanna--
-What are you birds sucking around for?

160
00:11:21,789 --> 00:11:24,643
Tell me or get out.

161
00:11:24,727 --> 00:11:28,024
You can't treat us like that.
It ain't right. We got our work to do.

162
00:11:28,098 --> 00:11:29,654
Why were you tailing Thursby?

163
00:11:29,731 --> 00:11:31,988
Miles was, and for the simple reason
we had a client--

164
00:11:32,102 --> 00:11:34,925
-Who's the client?
-Sorry, I can't tell you that.

165
00:11:34,993 --> 00:11:37,387
Be reasonable, Sam.
Give us a break, will you?

166
00:11:37,469 --> 00:11:40,764
How we gonna turn up anything
if you don't tell us what you got?

167
00:11:40,837 --> 00:11:42,570
Tom says you were in too much of a hurry

168
00:11:42,639 --> 00:11:44,835
to stop and look at your dead partner.

169
00:11:44,907 --> 00:11:47,363
And you didn't go to Archer's house
to tell his wife.

170
00:11:47,448 --> 00:11:51,074
We called your office and the girl there
said you told her to do it.

171
00:11:51,148 --> 00:11:54,147
I'll give you 10 minutes to get a phone
and do your talking to the girl.

172
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:56,603
I'll give you 10 minutes to get
to Thursby's. Geary and Leavenworth.

173
00:11:56,686 --> 00:12:00,719
-You could do it easily.

174
00:11:59,154 --> 00:12:00,812
-What's your boyfriend getting at, Tom?

175
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:03,873
Just this, Thursby was shot down
in front of his hotel...

176
00:12:03,957 --> 00:12:06,117
...about a half an hour
after you left Bush Street.

177
00:12:06,195 --> 00:12:07,455
-Keep your paws off me.

178
00:12:07,532 --> 00:12:09,261
-What time did you get home?

179
00:12:09,331 --> 00:12:12,126
Just a few minutes ahead of you.
I was walking around, thinking.

180
00:12:12,202 --> 00:12:13,997
We know you weren't here.
Where did you walk to?

181
00:12:14,067 --> 00:12:15,598
-Up Bush Street.
-You see anybody--?

182
00:12:15,674 --> 00:12:18,361
SPADE:
No. No witnesses.

183
00:12:18,637 --> 00:12:20,933
Well, I know where I stand, now.

184
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:23,237
Sorry I got up on my hind legs,

185
00:12:23,343 --> 00:12:25,226
but you
fellas trying to rope me made me nervous.

186
00:12:25,337 --> 00:12:28,302
Miles getting bumped off upset me,
and then you birds cracking foxy.

187
00:12:28,381 --> 00:12:30,713
It's all right,
now I know what it's all about.

188
00:12:30,781 --> 00:12:32,107
Forget it, Sam.

189
00:12:32,181 --> 00:12:33,581
-Thursby die?
-Yeah.

190
00:12:33,649 --> 00:12:35,048
How'd I kill him? I forget.

191
00:12:35,154 --> 00:12:39,710
He was shot in the back four times
with a .44 or .45 from across the street.

192
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:42,151
Nobody saw it, but that's how it figures.

193
00:12:42,222 --> 00:12:43,683
-Hotel people know anything?

194
00:12:43,764 --> 00:12:45,283
-Nothing, except that he lived there a week.

195
00:12:45,361 --> 00:12:46,851
-Alone?
-Yeah, alone.

196
00:12:46,958 --> 00:12:50,485
Did you find out who he was?
What his game was? Well, did you?

197
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:54,693
-We thought you could tell us that.
-I've never seen Thursby, dead or alive.

198
00:12:54,769 --> 00:12:56,165
Well, you know me, Spade.

199
00:12:56,238 --> 00:12:57,425
If you did it, or if you didn't...

200
00:12:57,508 --> 00:13:00,297
...you'll get a square deal
and most of the breaks.

201
00:13:00,372 --> 00:13:03,572
Don't know as I'd blame you much.
The man that killed your partner.

202
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:06,203
But that won't stop me from nailing you.

203
00:13:06,286 --> 00:13:07,332
Fair enough.

204
00:13:07,411 --> 00:13:10,770
But I'd feel better about it
if you'd have a drink with me.

205
00:13:18,887 --> 00:13:20,217
Success to crime.

206
00:13:40,814 --> 00:13:42,364
EFFIE:
She's in there.

207
00:13:43,081 --> 00:13:46,894
I told you to keep her away from me.
Yes, but you didn't tell me how.

208
00:13:47,144 --> 00:13:49,636
Oh, don't be cranky with me, Sam.
I've had her all night.

209
00:13:49,717 --> 00:13:51,740
Sorry, angel. I didn't mean--

210
00:13:52,511 --> 00:13:53,879
Hello, Iva.

211
00:14:01,396 --> 00:14:02,483
Oh, Sam.

212
00:14:05,695 --> 00:14:06,922
Darling.

213
00:14:07,229 --> 00:14:09,320
Effie take care of everything?

214
00:14:09,433 --> 00:14:10,729
I think so.

215
00:14:12,166 --> 00:14:14,624
Sam, did you kill him?

216
00:14:16,805 --> 00:14:19,328
Who put that bright idea in your head?

217
00:14:19,905 --> 00:14:23,436
Well, I thought you said
if it wasn't for Miles, you'd....

218
00:14:24,317 --> 00:14:26,072
Be kind to me, Sam.

219
00:14:34,449 --> 00:14:37,474
"You killed my husband, Sam.
Be kind to me."

220
00:14:48,631 --> 00:14:50,328
Don't, Iva. Don't.

221
00:14:50,674 --> 00:14:54,193
You shouldn't have come here today,
darling. You ought to be home.

222
00:14:54,272 --> 00:14:55,863
You'll come soon?

223
00:14:55,938 --> 00:14:57,495
Soon as I can.

224
00:14:57,842 --> 00:14:59,396
Goodbye, Iva.

225
00:15:20,527 --> 00:15:21,654
EFFIE:
Well?

226
00:15:22,198 --> 00:15:24,419
How did you and the widow make out?

227
00:15:24,489 --> 00:15:26,291
-She thinks I shot Miles.

228
00:15:26,362 --> 00:15:29,132
-So you could marry her?The cops think I killed Thursby,

229
00:15:27,676 --> 00:15:31,787
the guy
Miles was tailing for that Wonderly dame.

230
00:15:31,864 --> 00:15:33,325
Who do you think I shot?

231
00:15:33,399 --> 00:15:34,862
Are you going to marry Iva?

232
00:15:34,937 --> 00:15:37,860
Don't be silly.
I wish I'd never laid eyes on her.

233
00:15:38,942 --> 00:15:41,534
Do you suppose
she could have killed him?

234
00:15:42,106 --> 00:15:45,098
You're an angel.
A nice, rattlebrained little angel.

235
00:15:45,178 --> 00:15:47,097
-Oh, am I?
-Thanks, honey.

236
00:15:47,215 --> 00:15:50,014
Suppose I told you Iva
hadn't been home many minutes...

237
00:15:50,118 --> 00:15:53,072
...when I arrived to break the news
at 3:00 this morning?

238
00:15:53,147 --> 00:15:54,315
Are you telling me?

239
00:15:54,388 --> 00:15:56,647
She kept me waiting
while she undressed.

240
00:15:56,721 --> 00:15:59,709
Her clothes were on a chair where she'd
dumped them, hat and coat underneath.

241
00:15:59,793 --> 00:16:01,811
Her slip, on top, was still warm.

242
00:16:01,888 --> 00:16:04,456
She'd wrinkled the bed,
but the wrinkles weren't mashed down.

243
00:16:04,533 --> 00:16:07,383
You're a detective, darling,
but she didn't kill him.

244
00:16:07,502 --> 00:16:10,257
Do the police really think
you shot this what's-his-name?

245
00:16:10,333 --> 00:16:12,731
Do they? Look at me, Sam.

246
00:16:13,139 --> 00:16:14,529
You worry me.

247
00:16:14,703 --> 00:16:16,224
You think you know what you're doing,

248
00:16:16,341 --> 00:16:18,862
but you're too slick for your own good.

249
00:16:19,508 --> 00:16:22,497
Someday you're going to find it out.
Spade and Archer.

250
00:16:22,949 --> 00:16:24,567
Oh, yes, Miss Wonderly.

251
00:16:26,417 --> 00:16:27,542
Hello?

252
00:16:27,610 --> 00:16:29,480
Yeah, this is Sam Spade.

253
00:16:30,947 --> 00:16:32,110
Where?

254
00:16:32,189 --> 00:16:36,346
Coronet Apartments, California Avenue,
apartment 1001.

255
00:16:36,823 --> 00:16:39,186
What's the name? Miss LeBlanc.

256
00:16:40,062 --> 00:16:42,052
Okay, I'll be right over.

257
00:16:46,363 --> 00:16:48,753
Oh, have Miles' desk
moved out of the office...

258
00:16:48,835 --> 00:16:52,392
...and have "Spade and Archer" taken off
all the doors and windows...

259
00:16:52,466 --> 00:16:54,626
...and have "Samuel Spade" put on.

260
00:17:08,561 --> 00:17:10,142
Oh, come in, Mr. Spade.

261
00:17:13,319 --> 00:17:17,347
Everything's upside down.
I haven't finished unpacking. Sit down.

262
00:17:24,336 --> 00:17:25,786
Mr. Spade, I....

263
00:17:26,733 --> 00:17:29,724
I have a terrible,
terrible confession to make.

264
00:17:30,848 --> 00:17:32,966
That story I told you yesterday...

265
00:17:33,504 --> 00:17:34,993
...was just a story.

266
00:17:35,076 --> 00:17:38,530
Oh, that. Well, we didn't exactly
believe your story, Miss....

267
00:17:38,979 --> 00:17:41,202
What is your name, Wonderly or LeBlanc?

268
00:17:41,281 --> 00:17:43,433
It's really O'Shaughnessy.
Brigid O'Shaughnessy.

269
00:17:43,510 --> 00:17:46,001
We didn't believe your story,
Miss O'Shaughnessy.

270
00:17:46,085 --> 00:17:48,600
-We believed your $200.
-You mean that....

271
00:17:48,683 --> 00:17:51,072
You paid us more
than if you'd been telling the truth...

272
00:17:51,153 --> 00:17:53,387
...and enough more to make it all right.

273
00:17:53,461 --> 00:17:56,815
Tell me, Mr. Spade,
am I to blame for last night?

274
00:17:56,956 --> 00:17:59,051
You warned us
that Thursby was dangerous.

275
00:17:59,130 --> 00:18:01,183
Of course, you lied to us about your sister,

276
00:18:01,261 --> 00:18:02,923
but we didn't believe you.

277
00:18:02,997 --> 00:18:05,451
No, I wouldn't say that you were at fault.

278
00:18:05,535 --> 00:18:06,758
Thank you.

279
00:18:07,232 --> 00:18:09,421
Mr. Archer was so alive yesterday...

280
00:18:09,531 --> 00:18:11,902
-...so solid and hearty, and--
-Stop it.

281
00:18:12,005 --> 00:18:15,096
He knew what he was doing.
Those are the chances we take.

282
00:18:15,174 --> 00:18:16,300
Was he married?

283
00:18:16,375 --> 00:18:20,232
Yeah, with 10,000 insurance, no children
and a wife that didn't like him.

284
00:18:20,312 --> 00:18:22,736
-Please don't.
-That's the way it was.

285
00:18:22,821 --> 00:18:25,807
Anyway, there's no time for worrying
about that now.

286
00:18:25,888 --> 00:18:28,939
Out there's a flock of policemen
and assistant district attorneys...

287
00:18:29,019 --> 00:18:31,939
...running around with their noses
to the ground.

288
00:18:32,783 --> 00:18:35,273
Mr. Spade, do they know about me?

289
00:18:35,353 --> 00:18:38,085
Not yet. I've been stalling them
until I could see you.

290
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:39,420
Do they have to know?

291
00:18:39,498 --> 00:18:42,481
Can't you shield me
so I won't have to answer their questions?

292
00:18:42,568 --> 00:18:45,291
Maybe, but I gotta know
what it's all about.

293
00:18:46,100 --> 00:18:47,589
I can't tell you.

294
00:18:48,675 --> 00:18:50,365
I can't tell you now.

295
00:18:52,276 --> 00:18:54,159
I will later, when I can.

296
00:18:54,272 --> 00:18:56,562
You've got to trust me, Mr. Spade.

297
00:18:57,214 --> 00:18:59,542
Oh, I'm so alone and afraid.

298
00:19:00,404 --> 00:19:03,499
I've got nobody to help me
if you won't help me.

299
00:19:03,813 --> 00:19:05,449
Be generous, Mr. Spade.

300
00:19:05,850 --> 00:19:07,914
You're brave. You're strong.

301
00:19:07,991 --> 00:19:10,867
You can spare me
some of that courage and strength, surely.

302
00:19:10,951 --> 00:19:13,319
Help me, Mr. Spade.
I need help so badly.

303
00:19:13,394 --> 00:19:16,745
I have no right to ask you.
I know I haven't, but I do ask you.

304
00:19:16,821 --> 00:19:18,052
Help me.

305
00:19:18,835 --> 00:19:21,747
You won't need much of anybody's help.
You're good.

306
00:19:21,830 --> 00:19:25,090
It's chiefly your eyes, I think,
and that throb you get in your voice...

307
00:19:25,162 --> 00:19:28,260
...when you say things like,
"Be generous, Mr. Spade."

308
00:19:28,373 --> 00:19:29,852
I deserve that.

309
00:19:30,506 --> 00:19:34,899
But the lie was in the way I said it,
not at all in what I said.

310
00:19:36,614 --> 00:19:39,462
It's my own fault
if you can't believe me now.

311
00:19:40,989 --> 00:19:42,860
Now you are dangerous.

312
00:19:43,521 --> 00:19:46,279
But I'm not going to be able
to be of much help to you...

313
00:19:46,347 --> 00:19:48,243
...unless I've got some idea
what it's all about.

314
00:19:48,321 --> 00:19:52,773
For instance, I've gotta have some sort
of a line on your Floyd Thursby.

315
00:19:52,759 --> 00:19:54,247
I met him in the Orient.

316
00:19:54,324 --> 00:19:57,079
We came here from Hong Kong last week.
He promised to help me.

317
00:19:57,152 --> 00:19:59,575
He took advantage of my dependence
to betray me.

318
00:19:59,696 --> 00:20:02,689
Betray you? How?
Why did you want him shadowed?

319
00:20:03,339 --> 00:20:06,821
I wanted to find out how far he'd gone,
whom he was meeting, things like that.

320
00:20:06,905 --> 00:20:09,024
-Did he kill Archer?
-Certainly.

321
00:20:09,444 --> 00:20:13,363
He had a Luger in his shoulder holster.
Archer wasn't shot with a Luger.

322
00:20:14,607 --> 00:20:18,930
Mr. Spade, you don't think I had anything
to do with the death of Mr. Archer?

323
00:20:19,212 --> 00:20:20,506
-Did you?
-No.

324
00:20:20,579 --> 00:20:21,838
That's good.

325
00:20:21,909 --> 00:20:24,807
Floyd always carried an extra revolver
in his overcoat pocket.

326
00:20:24,886 --> 00:20:26,607
Why all the guns?

327
00:20:26,715 --> 00:20:28,157
He lived by them.

328
00:20:28,226 --> 00:20:30,613
The story is that
he first came to the Orient...

329
00:20:30,724 --> 00:20:33,520
...as bodyguard to a gambler
who'd had to leave the States.

330
00:20:33,599 --> 00:20:36,183
The gambler had disappeared. Floyd knew
about the disappearance.

331
00:20:36,259 --> 00:20:37,555
I don't know.

332
00:20:37,632 --> 00:20:41,225
I do know he always went heavily armed,
and that he never went to sleep...

333
00:20:41,301 --> 00:20:44,482
...without covering the floor around his bed
with crumpled newspapers...

334
00:20:44,559 --> 00:20:47,089
...so that nobody could come
silently into his room.

335
00:20:47,175 --> 00:20:48,996
You picked a nice sort of a playmate.

336
00:20:49,070 --> 00:20:52,137
Only that sort could have helped me,
if he'd been loyal.

337
00:20:52,213 --> 00:20:55,156
-How bad a spot are you actually in?
-Bad as could be.

338
00:20:55,238 --> 00:20:56,534
Physical danger?

339
00:20:56,609 --> 00:21:00,136
I'm not heroic. I don't think
there's anything worse than death.

340
00:21:00,215 --> 00:21:03,879
-Then it's that?
-It's that, as surely as we're sitting here.

341
00:21:05,283 --> 00:21:06,875
Unless you help me.

342
00:21:07,950 --> 00:21:10,569
Who killed Thursby, your enemies or his?

343
00:21:11,197 --> 00:21:12,560
I don't know.

344
00:21:12,888 --> 00:21:14,378
His, I suppose.

345
00:21:14,801 --> 00:21:16,814
I'm afraid. I don't know.

346
00:21:16,893 --> 00:21:19,419
This is hopeless.
I don't know what you want done.

347
00:21:19,503 --> 00:21:21,156
-I don't even know if you know.

348
00:21:21,228 --> 00:21:22,324
-You won't go to the police?

349
00:21:22,404 --> 00:21:25,254
Go to them? All I gotta do is stand still,
they'll be all over me.

350
00:21:25,340 --> 00:21:28,104
I'll tell them all I know,
and you'll take your chances.

351
00:21:28,172 --> 00:21:30,592
You've been patient with me.
You've tried to help me.

352
00:21:30,671 --> 00:21:32,594
It's useless and hopeless, I suppose.

353
00:21:32,672 --> 00:21:36,634
I do thank you for what you've done.
I'll have to take my chances.

354
00:21:40,514 --> 00:21:44,093
How much money have you got?
I've got about $500 left.

355
00:21:44,088 --> 00:21:45,483
Give it to me.

356
00:22:04,436 --> 00:22:07,157
-There's only $400 here.
-I had to keep some to live on.

357
00:22:07,232 --> 00:22:08,796
-Well, can't you get some more?
-No.

358
00:22:08,876 --> 00:22:10,929
Got anything
you can raise some money on?

359
00:22:11,011 --> 00:22:14,670
-I've got some furs and a little jewelry.
-You'll have to hock them.

360
00:22:21,851 --> 00:22:23,074
There you are.

361
00:22:23,154 --> 00:22:25,742
I'll be back as soon as I can
with the best news I can manage.

362
00:22:25,855 --> 00:22:28,151
I'll ring four times.
Long, short, long, short.

363
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:31,945
And no, you needn't bother
to come to the door. I'll let myself in.

364
00:22:40,772 --> 00:22:42,198
Anything stirring?

365
00:22:42,270 --> 00:22:43,725
Did you send the flowers?

366
00:22:43,831 --> 00:22:47,691
You're invaluable, darling. Say,
get my lawyer on the phone, will you?

367
00:23:03,154 --> 00:23:04,479
Hello, Sid?

368
00:23:05,021 --> 00:23:08,785
I think I'm going to have to tell a coroner
to go to blazes, Sid.

369
00:23:09,562 --> 00:23:12,352
Can I hide behind the sanctity
of my client's identity...

370
00:23:12,430 --> 00:23:15,721
...secrets and what-nots
all the same, priest or lawyer?

371
00:23:16,061 --> 00:23:18,086
Yeah, but Dundy's getting rambunctious

372
00:23:18,167 --> 00:23:20,181
and maybe it is a bit thick this time.

373
00:23:20,263 --> 00:23:22,962
Yeah. What'll it cost
to be on the safe side?

374
00:23:23,171 --> 00:23:25,761
Well, maybe it's worth it.
Okay, go ahead.

375
00:23:32,347 --> 00:23:33,499
Gardenia.

376
00:23:33,579 --> 00:23:35,408
Quick, darling, in with him.

377
00:23:38,518 --> 00:23:40,536
Will you come in, Mr. Cairo?

378
00:23:46,290 --> 00:23:48,172
Will you sit down, Mr. Cairo?

379
00:23:48,456 --> 00:23:49,976
Thank you, sir.

380
00:23:52,762 --> 00:23:55,355
Now, what can I do for you, Mr. Cairo?

381
00:23:55,436 --> 00:23:59,656
May a stranger offer condolences
for your partner's unfortunate death?

382
00:24:00,137 --> 00:24:01,196
Thanks.

383
00:24:01,272 --> 00:24:03,390
Is there, Mr. Spade,
as the newspapers imply...

384
00:24:03,473 --> 00:24:07,323
...a certain relationship
between that unfortunate happening...

385
00:24:07,405 --> 00:24:10,495
...and the death a little later
of the man Thursby?

386
00:24:12,812 --> 00:24:14,681
I beg your pardon. No.

387
00:24:15,148 --> 00:24:17,871
More than idle curiosity prompted
my question.

388
00:24:18,350 --> 00:24:20,842
See, Mr. Spade, I'm trying to recover...

389
00:24:21,658 --> 00:24:24,782
...an ornament that, shall we say,
has been mislaid.

390
00:24:25,822 --> 00:24:28,013
-Uh-huh.
-I thought and hoped you could assist me.

391
00:24:28,089 --> 00:24:30,014
The ornament is a statuette.

392
00:24:30,962 --> 00:24:32,983
A black figure of a bird.

393
00:24:33,068 --> 00:24:35,013
I am prepared to pay,
on behalf of the figure's rightful owner...

394
00:24:35,094 --> 00:24:39,810
...the sum of $5,000 for its recovery.

395
00:24:39,933 --> 00:24:43,165
I am prepared to promise that--
What is the phrase?

396
00:24:44,009 --> 00:24:45,835
No questions will be asked.

397
00:24:45,939 --> 00:24:48,034
$5,000 is a lot of money.

398
00:24:51,977 --> 00:24:53,309
Yes, Effie?

399
00:24:53,944 --> 00:24:57,511
No, that'll be all. Just be sure to lock
the door behind you on your way out.

400
00:24:57,589 --> 00:24:58,811
Good night.

401
00:24:59,975 --> 00:25:00,935
$5,000 is--

402
00:25:01,016 --> 00:25:04,647
Clasp your hands together
at the back of your neck.

403
00:25:04,826 --> 00:25:07,618
I intend to search your offices,
Mr. Spade.

404
00:25:08,230 --> 00:25:12,080
I warn you, if you attempt to prevent me,
I shall certainly shoot you.

405
00:25:12,830 --> 00:25:14,283
Go ahead and search.

406
00:25:14,363 --> 00:25:17,194
Will you please come
to the center of the room?

407
00:25:19,606 --> 00:25:22,455
Well, I have to make certain
that you are not armed.

408
00:26:48,947 --> 00:26:50,775
Look what you did to my shirt.

409
00:26:50,879 --> 00:26:52,002
Sorry.

410
00:26:52,080 --> 00:26:55,240
But imagine my embarrassment
when I found out that $5,000 offer...

411
00:26:55,315 --> 00:26:56,407
-...was just hooey.

412
00:26:56,482 --> 00:26:58,743
-Mr. Spade, my offer is genuine.

413
00:26:59,115 --> 00:27:02,577
I am prepared to pay $5,000
for the figure's return.

414
00:27:03,823 --> 00:27:05,414
-Do you have it?
-No.

415
00:27:06,630 --> 00:27:07,922
But if it isn't here...

416
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:11,044
...why did you risk injury
to prevent my searching for it?

417
00:27:11,127 --> 00:27:14,218
Why should I sit around here
and let people come in and stick me up?

418
00:27:14,298 --> 00:27:17,567
But certainly it is only natural
that I try to save the owner...

419
00:27:17,638 --> 00:27:19,794
...such a considerable expense,
if possible.

420
00:27:19,901 --> 00:27:21,061
Who is he?

421
00:27:21,141 --> 00:27:24,660
Mr. Spade, you'll forgive
my not answering that question.

422
00:27:24,744 --> 00:27:26,901
Well, I think we'd be better off all around

423
00:27:26,974 --> 00:27:28,412
if we'd put our cards on the table.

424
00:27:28,482 --> 00:27:31,037
No, I do not think it would be better.

425
00:27:31,514 --> 00:27:34,209
You see, Mr. Spade,
if you know more than I do...

426
00:27:34,288 --> 00:27:36,340
...then I shall profit by your knowledge.

427
00:27:36,418 --> 00:27:39,301
So will you, to the extent of $5,000.

428
00:27:39,389 --> 00:27:41,686
Well, there's nothing like $5,000 here.

429
00:27:41,758 --> 00:27:44,585
Oh, you want some assurance
of my sincerity?

430
00:27:44,760 --> 00:27:46,988
-A retainer? Would that do?
-It might.

431
00:27:47,094 --> 00:27:49,046
You will take, say, $100?

432
00:27:49,262 --> 00:27:51,463
No. I will take, say, $200.

433
00:27:52,097 --> 00:27:54,588
Let's see, your first guess was
that I had the bird.

434
00:27:54,669 --> 00:27:55,688
There's nothing to that.

435
00:27:55,769 --> 00:27:57,228
What's your second guess?

436
00:27:57,305 --> 00:28:01,363
That you know where it is, or at least
you know it is where you can get it.

437
00:28:01,437 --> 00:28:03,963
You're not hiring me to do any murders
or burglaries...

438
00:28:04,042 --> 00:28:07,372
...but simply to get it back if possible,
in an honest, lawful way?

439
00:28:07,440 --> 00:28:08,669
If possible.

440
00:28:08,742 --> 00:28:11,871
But in any case, with discretion.

441
00:28:13,776 --> 00:28:17,709
When you wish to contact me, sir,
I'm staying at the Hotel Belvedere.

442
00:28:17,789 --> 00:28:20,053
Room 635.

443
00:28:21,021 --> 00:28:24,045
I sincerely expect
the greatest mutual benefit...

444
00:28:24,297 --> 00:28:26,657
...from our association, Mr. Spade.

445
00:28:27,528 --> 00:28:29,353
Oh, may I please have my gun, now?

446
00:28:29,430 --> 00:28:31,884
Oh, sure. I'd forgotten all about it.

447
00:28:34,003 --> 00:28:37,194
Will you please clasp your hands together
at the back of your neck?

448
00:28:37,271 --> 00:28:39,430
I intend to search your offices.

449
00:28:39,503 --> 00:28:40,874
Well, I'll be.

450
00:28:44,246 --> 00:28:45,569
Why, sure.

451
00:28:47,017 --> 00:28:49,397
Go ahead. I won't stop you.

452
00:29:35,050 --> 00:29:38,219
-Turn to the right and go up the hill, driver.
-Okay.

453
00:30:18,251 --> 00:30:19,543
Mr. Spade.

454
00:30:21,123 --> 00:30:22,854
Do you bring any news?

455
00:30:22,990 --> 00:30:26,049
Did you manage it so that
the police won't have to know about me?

456
00:30:26,130 --> 00:30:27,857
Well, they won't for a while, anyway.

457
00:30:27,932 --> 00:30:29,525
You are wonderful.

458
00:30:30,237 --> 00:30:31,754
You won't get into any trouble, will you?

459
00:30:31,866 --> 00:30:34,568
SPADE: I don't mind a reasonable amount
of trouble.

460
00:30:36,880 --> 00:30:38,299
Do sit down.

461
00:30:48,930 --> 00:30:50,764
You, uh....

462
00:30:50,918 --> 00:30:54,579
You aren't exactly the sort of a person
you pretend to be, are you?

463
00:30:55,458 --> 00:30:58,382
Why, I'm not sure I know exactly
what you mean.

464
00:30:58,495 --> 00:31:02,187
The schoolgirl manner. You know,
blushing, stammering and all that.

465
00:31:04,827 --> 00:31:07,618
I haven't lived a good life. I've been bad.

466
00:31:09,166 --> 00:31:10,651
Worse than you could know.

467
00:31:10,732 --> 00:31:12,956
That's good, because if you actually
were as innocent as you pretend to be...

468
00:31:13,036 --> 00:31:15,827
...we'd never get anywhere.

469
00:31:15,905 --> 00:31:17,231
I won't be innocent.

470
00:31:17,307 --> 00:31:18,432
Good.

471
00:31:18,751 --> 00:31:23,255
Oh, by the way, I saw Joel Cairo tonight.

472
00:31:23,945 --> 00:31:26,371
-Do you know him?
-Only slightly.

473
00:31:51,572 --> 00:31:53,798
You're good. You're very good.

474
00:31:53,971 --> 00:31:55,533
-What did he say?
-About what?

475
00:31:55,606 --> 00:31:57,331
-About me.
-Nothing.

476
00:31:57,739 --> 00:32:01,766
-What did you talk about then?
-He offered me $5,000 for the black bird.

477
00:32:05,209 --> 00:32:07,540
You're not gonna go around straightening
things

478
00:32:07,616 --> 00:32:09,740
and poking the fire again, are you?

479
00:32:09,821 --> 00:32:11,145
No, I'm not.

480
00:32:11,953 --> 00:32:15,289
-What did you say?
-I said $5,000 was a lot of money.

481
00:32:15,360 --> 00:32:16,518
It is.

482
00:32:16,995 --> 00:32:20,810
It's more than I can ever offer you
if I have to bid for your loyalty.

483
00:32:22,798 --> 00:32:24,914
That's good coming from you.

484
00:32:25,557 --> 00:32:28,081
What have you ever given me
beside money?

485
00:32:28,165 --> 00:32:31,228
You ever given me any of your confidence,
any of the truth?

486
00:32:31,337 --> 00:32:33,792
Haven't you tried to buy my loyalty
with money and nothing else?

487
00:32:33,871 --> 00:32:36,294
What else is there I can buy you with?

488
00:32:52,889 --> 00:32:55,374
I don't care what your secrets are...

489
00:32:55,986 --> 00:32:59,660
...but I can't go ahead without
more confidence in you than I've got now.

490
00:32:59,732 --> 00:33:01,648
You've got to convince me that you aren't
fiddling around...

491
00:33:01,725 --> 00:33:05,107
...hoping it'll all come out right
in the end.

492
00:33:05,103 --> 00:33:06,293
-Can't you trust me a little longer?

493
00:33:06,404 --> 00:33:08,693
-What are you waiting for?

494
00:33:08,765 --> 00:33:10,428
I've got to talk to Joel Cairo.

495
00:33:10,505 --> 00:33:13,858
You can see him tonight.
He's at the theater. It'll be out soon.

496
00:33:13,936 --> 00:33:16,957
-I'll leave a message at his hotel.
-He can't come here.

497
00:33:17,038 --> 00:33:19,131
I can't let him know where I am.
I'm afraid.

498
00:33:19,208 --> 00:33:20,696
My place, then.

499
00:33:21,677 --> 00:33:22,804
Hello?

500
00:33:22,883 --> 00:33:25,335
-I want to leave a message for Joel Cairo.
-All right.

501
00:33:47,801 --> 00:33:50,185
I never would've placed myself in this
position

502
00:33:50,274 --> 00:33:52,133
if I didn't trust you completely.

503
00:33:52,234 --> 00:33:53,569
That again?

504
00:33:56,674 --> 00:33:58,557
Do you know that's true, though?

505
00:33:59,045 --> 00:34:03,174
You don't have to trust me, so long
as you can persuade me to trust you.

506
00:34:04,252 --> 00:34:06,441
But don't worry about that now.

507
00:34:06,515 --> 00:34:08,402
He'll be along any minute.

508
00:34:08,846 --> 00:34:12,412
You get your business with Cairo over with,
then we'll see how we stand.

509
00:34:12,492 --> 00:34:15,613
And you'll let me go about it with him
in my own way?

510
00:34:15,697 --> 00:34:16,886
SPADE:
Oh, sure.

511
00:34:19,660 --> 00:34:21,320
You are a godsend.

512
00:34:22,096 --> 00:34:24,087
Well, now don't overdo it.

513
00:34:32,143 --> 00:34:35,949
Mr. Spade, there is a boy outside.
He seems to be watching the house.

514
00:34:36,068 --> 00:34:37,369
Yeah, I know. I spotted him.

515
00:34:37,469 --> 00:34:39,429
What? What's that? What boy?

516
00:34:39,517 --> 00:34:42,066
I don't know. A kid.
He's been trailing me all evening.

517
00:34:42,148 --> 00:34:45,237
-Did he follow you to my apartment?
-I shook him long before that.

518
00:34:45,313 --> 00:34:47,049
Come in, Mr. Cairo.

519
00:34:51,823 --> 00:34:55,918
-I'm delighted to see you again, madam.
-I was sure you would be, Joel.

520
00:35:00,096 --> 00:35:03,117
Mr. Spade told me about your offer
for the falcon.

521
00:35:03,200 --> 00:35:05,463
-How soon can you have the money?
-The money is ready.

522
00:35:05,536 --> 00:35:06,799
O'SHAUGHNESSY: In cash?
-Oh, yes.

523
00:35:06,869 --> 00:35:09,825
You're ready to pay $5,000
if we turn over the falcon to you?

524
00:35:09,907 --> 00:35:12,795
Excuse me, please.
I must have expressed myself badly.

525
00:35:12,870 --> 00:35:15,464
I did not mean to say
that I have the money in my pocket...

526
00:35:15,546 --> 00:35:18,566
...but I am ready to get it for you
on a minute's notice...

527
00:35:18,646 --> 00:35:20,405
...anytime during banking hours.

528
00:35:20,509 --> 00:35:23,107
That's probably true.
He only had a couple of hundred...

529
00:35:23,190 --> 00:35:25,732
...when I searched him this afternoon
at my office.

530
00:35:25,813 --> 00:35:30,518
I shall be able to have the money for you
at, say, half past 10 in the morning.

531
00:35:31,662 --> 00:35:33,675
But I haven't got the falcon.

532
00:35:35,259 --> 00:35:38,052
I'll have it in another week
at the most, though.

533
00:35:38,164 --> 00:35:39,718
Then where is it?

534
00:35:39,831 --> 00:35:42,755
-Where Floyd hid it.
-Floyd hid....

535
00:35:43,168 --> 00:35:46,232
And you know where he hid it?
Then why do we have to wait a week?

536
00:35:46,342 --> 00:35:47,787
Oh, perhaps not a whole week.

537
00:35:47,869 --> 00:35:51,852
And why, if I may ask another question,
are you willing to sell it to me?

538
00:35:52,638 --> 00:35:54,399
Because I'm afraid.
After what happened to Floyd...

539
00:35:54,846 --> 00:35:56,892
...I'm afraid to touch it,

540
00:35:56,968 --> 00:35:58,504
except to turn it over to somebody.

541
00:35:58,581 --> 00:36:00,777
What exactly did happen to Floyd?

542
00:36:03,351 --> 00:36:04,677
The fat man.

543
00:36:05,154 --> 00:36:06,444
Fat man.

544
00:36:06,988 --> 00:36:09,708
-Is he here?
O'SHAUGHNESSY: I don't know.

545
00:36:08,916 --> 00:36:11,609
I suppose so.
What difference does it make?

546
00:36:11,690 --> 00:36:13,852
It might make a world of difference.

547
00:36:14,898 --> 00:36:16,121
Or you or me.

548
00:36:16,193 --> 00:36:19,283
Precisely. But, shall we add,

549
00:36:20,597 --> 00:36:21,893
more certainly the boy outside?

550
00:36:21,968 --> 00:36:25,386
Yes. But you might be able
to get around him, Joel...

551
00:36:25,468 --> 00:36:27,386
...as you did the one in Istanbul.

552
00:36:27,468 --> 00:36:29,130
What was his name?

553
00:36:29,474 --> 00:36:32,170
You mean the one you couldn't get
to come to--

554
00:36:36,550 --> 00:36:39,776
This is the second time
that you have laid hands on me.

555
00:36:39,851 --> 00:36:42,551
When you're slapped,
you'll take it and like it.

556
00:36:45,020 --> 00:36:46,380
Who's that?

557
00:36:46,584 --> 00:36:48,416
I don't know. Keep quiet.

558
00:36:56,396 --> 00:36:57,553
SPADE:
Hello.

559
00:36:58,230 --> 00:37:00,788
You guys pick swell hours
to do your visiting in.

560
00:37:00,860 --> 00:37:03,127
-What is it?
-We want to talk to you, Spade.

561
00:37:03,208 --> 00:37:04,365
-Go ahead and talk.

562
00:37:04,440 --> 00:37:06,057
-We don't have to do it in the hall, do we?

563
00:37:06,132 --> 00:37:09,194
-You can't come in.
-Oh, come off it now, Sam.

564
00:37:09,605 --> 00:37:11,729
You aren't trying to strong-arm me,
are you, Tom?

565
00:37:11,811 --> 00:37:13,294
Why don't you be reasonable?

566
00:37:13,409 --> 00:37:15,469
It would pay you to play along with us.

567
00:37:15,576 --> 00:37:17,105
You got away with this and that...

568
00:37:17,215 --> 00:37:18,810
...but you can't keep it up.

569
00:37:18,881 --> 00:37:21,759
-Stop me when you can.
-That's what I intend to do.

570
00:37:21,847 --> 00:37:24,208
There's talk going around
about you and Archer's wife.

571
00:37:24,282 --> 00:37:26,444
-Is there anything to it?
-Not a thing.

572
00:37:26,529 --> 00:37:28,474
The talk is she tried to get a divorce

573
00:37:28,587 --> 00:37:30,750
so she
could put in with you. He wouldn't give it.

574
00:37:30,827 --> 00:37:32,190
-Anything to that?
-No.

575
00:37:32,261 --> 00:37:34,321
There's talk that that's why
he was put on the spot.

576
00:37:34,431 --> 00:37:36,012
Don't be a hog, Dundy.

577
00:37:36,094 --> 00:37:38,820
Your idea that I killed Thursby
because he killed Miles...

578
00:37:38,895 --> 00:37:41,264
...falls to pieces if you blame me
for killing Miles.

579
00:37:41,332 --> 00:37:43,385
You haven't heard me say
you killed anybody.

580
00:37:43,469 --> 00:37:44,830
-You're the one bringing that up.

581
00:37:44,903 --> 00:37:47,561
-Haven't you anything better to do than to keep popping in here every
morning,

582
00:37:47,640 --> 00:37:50,018
asking fool questions?

583
00:37:50,101 --> 00:37:53,136
-And getting a lot of lying answers.
-Take it easy.

584
00:37:53,246 --> 00:37:55,198
If you say there's nothing between
you and Archer's wife,

585
00:37:55,278 --> 00:37:57,007
you're a liar.

586
00:37:57,078 --> 00:38:00,677
Is that the hot tip that brought you
up here at this ungodly hour?

587
00:38:00,756 --> 00:38:01,972
-That's one of them.

588
00:38:02,049 --> 00:38:03,485
-And the other?

589
00:38:03,980 --> 00:38:05,213
Let us in.

590
00:38:07,128 --> 00:38:09,075
All right, Spade, we'll go.

591
00:38:09,693 --> 00:38:12,417
Maybe you're right in bucking us.
Think it over.

592
00:38:15,196 --> 00:38:16,292
[THUDDING]
CAIRO:
Help!

593
00:38:16,729 --> 00:38:19,457
-I guess we're going in.
-I guess you are.

594
00:38:22,207 --> 00:38:23,663
DUNDY:
What's going on in here?

595
00:38:23,736 --> 00:38:26,192
CAIRO:
Look. Look, officer. Look what she did.

596
00:38:27,073 --> 00:38:28,956
-Did you do that?
O'SHAUGHNESSY: I had to.

597
00:38:29,071 --> 00:38:31,099
I was alone with him.
He tried to attack me.

598
00:38:31,179 --> 00:38:33,166
I couldn't bring myself to shoot him.

599
00:38:33,273 --> 00:38:36,232
You dirty, filthy liar, you. It isn't true.

600
00:38:36,311 --> 00:38:39,270
I came up here in good faith,
and then both of them attacked me.

601
00:38:39,347 --> 00:38:42,880
And then when he went out to talk to you,
he left her here with a pistol.

602
00:38:42,950 --> 00:38:45,318
She said as soon as you leave,
they are going to kill me...

603
00:38:45,393 --> 00:38:48,746
...so I called for help
because I didn't want you to leave.

604
00:38:48,827 --> 00:38:51,024
And then she struck me with a pistol.

605
00:38:51,098 --> 00:38:53,390
O'SHAUGHNESSY:
Why don't you make him tell the truth?

606
00:38:54,225 --> 00:38:55,454
CAIRO: Look. Look what she....
-Behave yourself, sister.

607
00:38:55,531 --> 00:38:56,968
That's no way to act.

608
00:38:57,035 --> 00:38:59,690
I don't guess we'll be wrong
running the lot of them in.

609
00:38:59,760 --> 00:39:02,286
Don't be in a hurry.
Everything can be explained.

610
00:39:02,358 --> 00:39:03,487
I'll bet.

611
00:39:03,561 --> 00:39:04,930
Miss O'Shaughnessy, may I present

612
00:39:05,003 --> 00:39:07,499
Lieutenant Dundy and Detective Polhaus?

613
00:39:07,576 --> 00:39:11,366
Miss O'Shaughnessy is an operative
in my employ since yesterday.

614
00:39:11,446 --> 00:39:12,573
That's a lie too.

615
00:39:12,683 --> 00:39:14,268
That is Mr. Joel Cairo.

616
00:39:14,688 --> 00:39:18,580
Cairo was an acquaintance of Thursby's.
He came into my office late this afternoon...

617
00:39:18,674 --> 00:39:20,337
...hired me to find something that

618
00:39:20,419 --> 00:39:22,643
Thursby was supposed to have on him.

619
00:39:22,717 --> 00:39:26,045
It looked funny to me the way he put it,
so I wouldn't touch it.

620
00:39:26,117 --> 00:39:29,154
Then he pulled a gun on me.
Well, that's neither here nor there...

621
00:39:29,231 --> 00:39:32,383
...unless we start preferring charges
against each other.

622
00:39:33,528 --> 00:39:35,650
Miss O'Shaughnessy and I
discussed the matter...

623
00:39:35,723 --> 00:39:38,216
...and decided to find out
how much he knew...

624
00:39:38,331 --> 00:39:40,119
...about Miles' and Thursby's killings,

625
00:39:40,196 --> 00:39:42,130
so we asked him to come up here.

626
00:39:42,211 --> 00:39:45,625
Now, maybe we did put the questions
to him a little roughly.

627
00:39:46,007 --> 00:39:47,893
You know how that is, lieutenant.

628
00:39:47,975 --> 00:39:50,803
But we didn't hurt him enough
to make him cry for help.

629
00:39:50,878 --> 00:39:53,869
-What have you got to say to that?
-I don't know what to say.

630
00:39:53,949 --> 00:39:55,344
Try telling the facts.

631
00:39:55,416 --> 00:39:57,370
-What? Facts?
-Oh, quit stalling.

632
00:39:57,452 --> 00:40:00,210
All you have to do is swear out
a complaint

633
00:40:00,281 --> 00:40:01,611
and I'll throw them in the can.

634
00:40:01,711 --> 00:40:03,675
Go ahead, Cairo. Tell him, he'll do it.

635
00:40:03,758 --> 00:40:07,605
Then we'll swear out a complaint
against you, and he'll have the lot of us.

636
00:40:07,719 --> 00:40:09,219
Get your hats.

637
00:40:09,497 --> 00:40:12,654
Well, boys and girls,
we put it over nicely.

638
00:40:12,767 --> 00:40:13,986
-Go on, get your hats.

639
00:40:14,064 --> 00:40:15,855
-Don't you know when you're being kidded?

640
00:40:15,927 --> 00:40:18,290
No, but that can wait
till we get down to the hall.

641
00:40:18,363 --> 00:40:20,813
Wake up, Dundy, you're being kidded.

642
00:40:20,927 --> 00:40:23,765
When I heard the buzzer, I said
to Miss O'Shaughnessy and Cairo:

643
00:40:23,840 --> 00:40:26,967
"There's the police.
They're getting to be a nuisance.

644
00:40:27,043 --> 00:40:28,527
When you hear them going, scream.

645
00:40:28,606 --> 00:40:31,603
We'll see how far we can string them
until they tumble."

646
00:40:31,678 --> 00:40:33,169
Stop it, Sam.

647
00:40:33,254 --> 00:40:35,375
That cut on his head.
How did that get there?

648
00:40:35,452 --> 00:40:38,499
-Ask him. Maybe he cut himself shaving.
-The cut? No.

649
00:40:38,584 --> 00:40:42,900
When we pretended to be struggling
for the gun, I fell over the carpet. I fell.

650
00:40:43,047 --> 00:40:44,268
Horsefeathers.

651
00:40:44,382 --> 00:40:46,086
Take him along, anyway,
for packing a gun.

652
00:40:46,158 --> 00:40:47,643
Don't be a sap.

653
00:40:47,852 --> 00:40:49,687
That gun was a plant.
It was one of mine.

654
00:40:49,792 --> 00:40:52,744
Too bad it was only a .25.
You could prove that was the gun...

655
00:40:52,829 --> 00:40:55,297
...Miles and Thursby were shot with.

656
00:40:56,439 --> 00:40:58,484
-No, Sam. No.
-Well, then get him out of here.

657
00:40:58,595 --> 00:41:02,506
-Get their names and addresses.
-My name is Joel Cairo, Hotel Belvedere.

658
00:41:02,618 --> 00:41:04,288
Miss O'Shaughnessy's address
is my office.

659
00:41:04,396 --> 00:41:06,326
-Where do you live?
-Get him out of here.

660
00:41:06,409 --> 00:41:08,960
Take it easy, will you?
Is that all you want?

661
00:41:09,040 --> 00:41:10,488
-Yeah.
-I think I'll be going now.

662
00:41:10,601 --> 00:41:11,977
What's the hurry, Cairo?

663
00:41:12,047 --> 00:41:15,132
There is no hurry.
It's getting quite late, and....

664
00:41:16,693 --> 00:41:18,598
Tell him to leave the gun.

665
00:41:22,022 --> 00:41:24,713
I hope you know
what you're doing, Sam.

666
00:41:34,634 --> 00:41:38,622
You're absolutely the wildest, most
unpredictable person I've ever known.

667
00:41:38,832 --> 00:41:41,283
Do you always carry on so high-handed?

668
00:41:43,210 --> 00:41:45,507
Well, you've had your talk with Cairo.

669
00:41:45,941 --> 00:41:47,635
Now you can talk to me.

670
00:41:48,370 --> 00:41:49,900
Oh, yes, of course.

671
00:41:50,146 --> 00:41:51,670
I'm listening.

672
00:41:53,014 --> 00:41:54,498
I'm still listening.

673
00:41:54,576 --> 00:41:58,903
-Oh, look at the time. I must be going.
-Oh, no. Not till you've told me all about it.

674
00:41:59,087 --> 00:42:00,607
Am I a prisoner?

675
00:42:01,055 --> 00:42:03,542
Maybe the boy outside
hasn't gone home yet.

676
00:42:05,323 --> 00:42:07,248
Do you suppose he's still there?

677
00:42:07,325 --> 00:42:08,549
Likely.

678
00:42:09,657 --> 00:42:11,150
You can start now.

679
00:42:15,866 --> 00:42:18,289
You are the most insistent person.

680
00:42:18,904 --> 00:42:20,931
And wild and unpredictable, huh?

681
00:42:21,773 --> 00:42:25,586
Say, what's this bird, this falcon
that everybody's all steamed up about?

682
00:42:25,667 --> 00:42:28,163
Supposing I wouldn't tell you
anything about it at all?

683
00:42:28,238 --> 00:42:31,366
What would you do?
Something wild and unpredictable?

684
00:42:31,875 --> 00:42:32,966
I might.

685
00:42:35,382 --> 00:42:37,940
It's a black figure, as you know,
smooth and shiny...

686
00:42:38,016 --> 00:42:40,748
...of a bird, a hawk or falcon,
about that high.

687
00:42:41,057 --> 00:42:42,119
Oh, here.

688
00:42:42,768 --> 00:42:48,481
-What--? What makes it so important?
-I don't know. They wouldn't tell me.

689
00:42:48,227 --> 00:42:52,146
They offered me ￡500 if I'd help them
get it away from the man who had it.

690
00:42:52,223 --> 00:42:53,751
That was in Istanbul?

691
00:42:53,867 --> 00:42:55,062
Marmara.

692
00:42:55,797 --> 00:42:56,956
Go ahead.

693
00:42:57,237 --> 00:42:58,528
But that's all.

694
00:42:59,300 --> 00:43:02,431
They promised me ￡500 if I'd help them,
and I did.

695
00:43:03,211 --> 00:43:06,229
Then we found out that Joel Cairo
intended to desert us...

696
00:43:06,308 --> 00:43:09,442
...taking the falcon with him
and leaving Floyd and me nothing.

697
00:43:09,513 --> 00:43:11,611
So we did exactly that to him.

698
00:43:12,119 --> 00:43:14,378
Then I wasn't any better off
than before.

699
00:43:14,442 --> 00:43:16,832
Floyd hadn't any intention
of keeping his promise...

700
00:43:16,919 --> 00:43:20,045
...about sharing.
I learned that by the time we got here.

701
00:43:20,120 --> 00:43:21,843
What's the bird made of?

702
00:43:21,919 --> 00:43:25,653
Porcelain or black stone. I don't know.
I only saw it for a few minutes.

703
00:43:25,729 --> 00:43:28,447
Floyd showed it to me
when we first got hold of it.

704
00:43:28,528 --> 00:43:30,019
You are a liar.

705
00:43:33,231 --> 00:43:34,324
I am.

706
00:43:35,265 --> 00:43:36,966
I've always been a liar.

707
00:43:37,768 --> 00:43:39,886
Don't brag about it.

708
00:43:40,605 --> 00:43:42,964
Was there any truth at all in that yarn?

709
00:43:43,373 --> 00:43:44,532
Some.

710
00:43:45,211 --> 00:43:46,661
Not very much.

711
00:43:47,916 --> 00:43:50,370
Well, we got all night.

712
00:43:51,110 --> 00:43:54,208
Coffee'll be ready soon.
We'll have a cup and try again.

713
00:43:54,281 --> 00:43:56,079
Oh, I'm so tired.

714
00:43:57,653 --> 00:44:00,638
So tired of lying and making up lies.

715
00:44:01,126 --> 00:44:04,646
Not knowing what is a lie
and what's the truth. I wish....

716
00:44:27,579 --> 00:44:30,035
I want to talk to Mr. Cairo, Joel Cairo.

717
00:44:34,950 --> 00:44:36,040
Oh. Thanks.

718
00:44:47,800 --> 00:44:49,055
Where is he?

719
00:44:50,434 --> 00:44:52,590
-What?
-Where is he?

720
00:44:52,737 --> 00:44:54,652
-Who?
-Cairo.

721
00:44:55,061 --> 00:44:57,226
What do you think you're doing, Jack,
kidding me?

722
00:44:57,307 --> 00:44:58,995
I'll tell you when I am.

723
00:44:59,273 --> 00:45:00,859
New York, aren't you?

724
00:45:00,973 --> 00:45:02,162
Shove off.

725
00:45:02,441 --> 00:45:04,775
You're gonna have to talk to me
before you're through.

726
00:45:04,841 --> 00:45:08,103
Some of you will,
and you can tell the fat man I said so.

727
00:45:09,345 --> 00:45:12,478
Keep asking for it
and you're gonna get it. Plenty.

728
00:45:13,286 --> 00:45:14,984
I told you to shove off.

729
00:45:15,388 --> 00:45:16,577
Shove off.

730
00:45:18,024 --> 00:45:20,250
People lose teeth talking like that.

731
00:45:20,327 --> 00:45:22,919
You wanna hang around,
you'll be polite.

732
00:45:28,529 --> 00:45:29,583
Luke.

733
00:45:35,439 --> 00:45:37,322
-Hello, Sam.
-Hello, Luke.

734
00:45:37,406 --> 00:45:39,824
Say, that was too bad about Miles.

735
00:45:40,039 --> 00:45:41,839
Yeah. It was a tough break.

736
00:45:41,914 --> 00:45:43,793
I want to show you something.

737
00:45:46,880 --> 00:45:49,541
What do you let these cheap gunmen
hang around for...

738
00:45:49,651 --> 00:45:52,304
...with their heaters
bulging in their clothes?

739
00:45:55,086 --> 00:45:56,674
What do you want here?

740
00:46:00,392 --> 00:46:02,346
Well, if you don't want anything, beat it

741
00:46:02,428 --> 00:46:04,057
and don't come back.

742
00:46:06,465 --> 00:46:08,152
I won't forget you guys.

743
00:46:17,973 --> 00:46:21,068
-What is it?
-I don't know. I just spotted him.

744
00:46:22,041 --> 00:46:25,205
-Say, what about Miles?
-I'll see you, Luke.

745
00:46:26,152 --> 00:46:27,579
- 603
-Six-oh-three.
-Yes, sir.

746
00:46:29,882 --> 00:46:31,072
Good morning.

747
00:46:31,288 --> 00:46:32,476
-Good morning.
-There you are.

748
00:46:32,557 --> 00:46:34,211
Let's go someplace where we can talk.

749
00:46:34,290 --> 00:46:36,549
Our conversations have not been
such that

750
00:46:36,623 --> 00:46:38,176
I'm anxious to continue them.

751
00:46:38,262 --> 00:46:40,712
Forgive my speaking so bluntly,
but it is the truth.

752
00:46:40,792 --> 00:46:42,214
You mean last night?

753
00:46:42,286 --> 00:46:44,181
I had to throw in with her.

754
00:46:44,260 --> 00:46:45,816
I don't know where the bird is. She does.

755
00:46:45,928 --> 00:46:47,553
How we gonna get it if I don't play along?

756
00:46:47,634 --> 00:46:50,288
You always have a very smooth
explanation ready, huh?

757
00:46:50,365 --> 00:46:52,322
What do you want me to do?
Learn to stutter?

758
00:46:52,401 --> 00:46:55,067
Dundy take you to the station?
How long they work on you?

759
00:46:55,139 --> 00:46:56,864
Till a little while ago.

760
00:46:56,939 --> 00:47:00,293
-What'd they shake out of you?
-Shake out? Not one thing.

761
00:47:00,373 --> 00:47:03,434
I adhered to the course you indicated
earlier in your rooms...

762
00:47:03,513 --> 00:47:06,930
...but I certainly wish you would have
invented a more reasonable story.

763
00:47:07,015 --> 00:47:09,278
I felt distinctly like an idiot repeating it.

764
00:47:09,344 --> 00:47:11,577
Don't worry about the story's goofiness.

765
00:47:11,653 --> 00:47:14,309
A sensible one would have
had us all in the cooler.

766
00:47:14,387 --> 00:47:16,948
-You sure you didn't tell them anything?
-I did not.

767
00:47:17,025 --> 00:47:18,181
Now, you'll want sleep

768
00:47:18,254 --> 00:47:20,985
if you've been
standing up under a police grilling all night.

769
00:47:21,093 --> 00:47:23,087
See you later.

770
00:47:24,967 --> 00:47:26,843
No, not yet.

771
00:47:26,634 --> 00:47:29,469
Yes, I'll have him call you
the minute he comes in.

772
00:47:29,533 --> 00:47:32,089
That's the third time she's called
this morning.

773
00:47:32,166 --> 00:47:34,554
-Miss O'Shaughnessy's in there.
-Anything else?

774
00:47:34,635 --> 00:47:38,296
The district attorney's office called.
Bryan would like to see you.

775
00:47:38,373 --> 00:47:40,169
And a Mr. Gutman called.
When I told him you weren't in...

776
00:47:40,236 --> 00:47:43,484
...he said, "Tell him the young man...

777
00:47:42,944 --> 00:47:47,071
...gave me his message,
and that I phoned and will phone again?"

778
00:47:47,776 --> 00:47:48,972
Gutman, huh?

779
00:47:50,252 --> 00:47:51,615
Thanks, darling.

780
00:47:53,558 --> 00:47:55,975
Darling. Somebody's been in my apartment.

781
00:47:56,055 --> 00:47:57,858
It's all upside down, every which way.

782
00:47:57,958 --> 00:48:00,188
I changed as fast as I could
and came right over here.

783
00:48:00,256 --> 00:48:01,979
-You must have let that boy follow you.
-No, angel.

784
00:48:02,054 --> 00:48:06,299
I shook him off
long before I ever went to your place.

785
00:48:05,999 --> 00:48:09,021
It might have been Cairo.
He wasn't at the hotel last night.

786
00:48:09,131 --> 00:48:12,326
He told me he'd been standing up
under a police grilling. I wonder.

787
00:48:12,404 --> 00:48:14,289
-You saw Joel this morning?
-Yeah.

788
00:48:14,369 --> 00:48:15,489
Why?

789
00:48:15,570 --> 00:48:17,869
Because I've gotta keep
in some sort of touch...

790
00:48:17,972 --> 00:48:22,394
...with all the loose ends of this dizzy affair
if I'm ever gonna make heads or tails of it.

791
00:48:22,476 --> 00:48:24,711
Now we've got to find
a new home for you.

792
00:48:24,780 --> 00:48:26,640
I won't go back there.

793
00:48:28,418 --> 00:48:30,435
I got an idea. Wait a minute.

794
00:48:33,322 --> 00:48:35,475
What does your woman's intuition
tell you about her?

795
00:48:35,556 --> 00:48:38,581
She's all right. Maybe it's her own fault

796
00:48:38,658 --> 00:48:40,248
for being in trouble, but she's all right.

797
00:48:40,326 --> 00:48:41,313
Are you strong enough for her

798
00:48:41,399 --> 00:48:43,459
to put her up for a few days?

799
00:48:43,532 --> 00:48:45,226
-You mean at home?
-Yeah.

800
00:48:45,368 --> 00:48:48,093
-Is she in any danger, Sam?
-I think she is.

801
00:48:48,434 --> 00:48:50,763
Gee, that'd scare Mom
into a green hemorrhage.

802
00:48:50,830 --> 00:48:52,955
I'd have to say she's a surprise witness

803
00:48:53,033 --> 00:48:54,362
you're keeping undercover.

804
00:48:54,436 --> 00:48:55,799
You're a darling.

805
00:48:56,644 --> 00:48:57,835
Oh, Brigid.

806
00:48:58,639 --> 00:49:00,666
Effie offered to put you up
for a few days.

807
00:49:00,741 --> 00:49:01,970
That's very kind.

808
00:49:02,045 --> 00:49:04,176
You'd better start now. Go out the back.

809
00:49:04,251 --> 00:49:06,228
There's usually a cab parked there.

810
00:49:06,309 --> 00:49:09,012
Ride partway with her
and make sure you're not followed.

811
00:49:09,085 --> 00:49:11,349
Change cabs a couple of times,
just to be safe.

812
00:49:11,415 --> 00:49:12,514
I'll give Mom a ring.

813
00:49:12,582 --> 00:49:15,280
There's time enough for that
when you get back.

814
00:49:15,586 --> 00:49:17,079
I'll call you later.

815
00:49:37,845 --> 00:49:38,827
Hello?

816
00:49:39,146 --> 00:49:40,702
This is Samuel Spade.

817
00:49:40,851 --> 00:49:43,763
Say, my secretary tells me
Mr. Bryan wants to see me.

818
00:49:45,145 --> 00:49:47,977
Yeah. Ask him what time's
most convenient for him.

819
00:49:48,551 --> 00:49:51,247
Spade. S-P-A-D-E.

820
00:49:51,719 --> 00:49:53,048
Hello, honey.

821
00:49:53,255 --> 00:49:54,347
Yeah.

822
00:49:54,591 --> 00:49:56,613
Two-thirty. All right. Thanks.

823
00:49:57,219 --> 00:49:59,711
Oh, Sam, forgive me. Please forgive me.

824
00:50:00,398 --> 00:50:02,657
I sent those policemen
to your place last night.

825
00:50:02,733 --> 00:50:05,892
I was mad, crazy with jealousy.

826
00:50:04,918 --> 00:50:08,421
I phoned that if they went there
they'd learn something about Miles' murder.

827
00:50:08,136 --> 00:50:11,432
-What made you think of that?
-I was mad. I wanted to hurt you.

828
00:50:11,501 --> 00:50:12,958
Did you tell them who you were?

829
00:50:13,070 --> 00:50:15,335
-No. Sam, dearest, I--
-Where'd you phone from?

830
00:50:15,407 --> 00:50:17,169
-The drugstore across from your place.

831
00:50:17,274 --> 00:50:19,796
-You better hurry home...and think of something
to tell the police.

832
00:50:18,807 --> 00:50:22,209
And by the way...

833
00:50:22,277 --> 00:50:24,968
...where were you the night
Miles was murdered?

834
00:50:25,450 --> 00:50:26,574
Home.

835
00:50:27,249 --> 00:50:28,474
-I was.
-No.

836
00:50:28,551 --> 00:50:31,384
But if that's your story,
it's all right with me.

837
00:50:32,156 --> 00:50:33,814
Now you run along.

838
00:50:44,304 --> 00:50:45,354
Hello?

839
00:50:45,432 --> 00:50:47,228
Yeah, this is Spade.

840
00:50:47,299 --> 00:50:50,183
Oh, yes, Mr. Gutman, I got it.
I've been waiting to hear from you.

841
00:50:50,301 --> 00:50:53,827
Well, now. The sooner, the better.
Say, 15 minutes? Right.

842
00:50:54,100 --> 00:50:55,198
Twelve-C.

843
00:50:56,445 --> 00:50:58,064
Twelve-C to your left, sir.

844
00:51:17,558 --> 00:51:20,517
-Mr. Spade.
-How do you do, Mr. Gutman?

845
00:51:36,843 --> 00:51:38,040
You begin well, sir.

846
00:51:38,143 --> 00:51:40,343
I distrust a man who says when.

847
00:51:40,424 --> 00:51:42,743
He's got to be careful
not to drink too much...

848
00:51:42,817 --> 00:51:45,110
...because he's not to be trusted
when he does.

849
00:51:45,183 --> 00:51:46,343
Well, sir...

850
00:51:47,953 --> 00:51:50,842
...here's to plain speaking
and clear understanding.

851
00:51:59,462 --> 00:52:02,260
-You're a close-mouthed man?
-No, I like to talk.

852
00:52:02,363 --> 00:52:05,489
Better and better.
I distrust a close-mouthed man.

853
00:52:05,998 --> 00:52:09,562
He generally picks the wrong time to talk
and says the wrong things.

854
00:52:09,641 --> 00:52:13,768
Talking's something you can't do
judiciously unless you keep in practice.

855
00:52:15,477 --> 00:52:17,610
Now, sir. We'll talk, if you like.

856
00:52:17,787 --> 00:52:21,972
I'll tell you right out. I am a man who likes
talking to a man who likes to talk.

857
00:52:22,049 --> 00:52:23,100
Swell.

858
00:52:23,180 --> 00:52:25,544
Will we talk about the black bird?

859
00:52:26,250 --> 00:52:27,805
[CHUCKLES]
You're a man for me, sir.

860
00:52:27,886 --> 00:52:30,514
No beating about the bush.
Right to the point.

861
00:52:30,624 --> 00:52:32,883
Let's talk about the black bird,
by all means.

862
00:52:32,992 --> 00:52:34,520
But first, answer me a question.

863
00:52:34,596 --> 00:52:37,481
Are you here
as Miss O'Shaughnessy's representative?

864
00:52:37,696 --> 00:52:40,147
Well, there's nothing certain either way.
It depends.

865
00:52:40,232 --> 00:52:41,492
Depends on?

866
00:52:42,366 --> 00:52:44,284
Maybe it depends on Joel Cairo.

867
00:52:44,402 --> 00:52:45,455
Maybe.

868
00:52:45,540 --> 00:52:48,664
The question is then which you represent.
It'll be one or the other.

869
00:52:48,741 --> 00:52:50,257
I didn't say so.

870
00:52:51,210 --> 00:52:53,226
-Who else is there?
-There's me.

871
00:52:54,254 --> 00:52:57,840
Ah. Ha-ha.
That's wonderful, sir, wonderful.

872
00:52:57,911 --> 00:53:01,409
I do like a man who tells you right out
he's looking out for himself.

873
00:53:01,489 --> 00:53:02,936
Don't we all?

874
00:53:03,051 --> 00:53:05,349
I don't trust a man who says he's not.

875
00:53:05,682 --> 00:53:09,143
Uh-huh. Now, let's talk
about the black bird.

876
00:53:09,155 --> 00:53:10,249
Let's.

877
00:53:10,662 --> 00:53:12,924
Mr. Spade, have you any conception...

878
00:53:13,324 --> 00:53:16,241
...of how much money can be got
for that black bird?

879
00:53:16,492 --> 00:53:17,509
No.

880
00:53:17,688 --> 00:53:21,921
Well, sir, if I told you,
if I told you half, you'd call me a liar.

881
00:53:22,034 --> 00:53:23,758
No, not even if I thought so.

882
00:53:23,831 --> 00:53:27,097
But you tell me what it is,
and I'll figure out the profit.

883
00:53:30,444 --> 00:53:32,631
You mean, you don't know
what the bird is?

884
00:53:32,713 --> 00:53:35,155
Oh, I know
what it's supposed to look like...

885
00:53:35,244 --> 00:53:38,343
...and I know the value in human life
you people put on it.

886
00:53:38,454 --> 00:53:40,564
She didn't tell you what it is?

887
00:53:41,409 --> 00:53:44,647
-Cairo didn't, either?
-He offered me 10,000 for it.

888
00:53:44,722 --> 00:53:45,949
Ten thousand.

889
00:53:46,445 --> 00:53:48,612
Dollars, mind you, not even pounds.

890
00:53:49,423 --> 00:53:52,262
Do they know what that bird is, sir?
What is your impression?

891
00:53:52,331 --> 00:53:53,778
Not much to go by.

892
00:53:53,860 --> 00:53:55,722
Cairo didn't say he did, didn't say he didn't.

893
00:53:55,795 --> 00:53:59,093
She said she didn't,
but I took it for granted she was lying.

894
00:53:59,163 --> 00:54:01,154
Not an injudicious thing to do.

895
00:54:02,502 --> 00:54:06,725
If they don't know, I'm the only one
in the whole wide sweet world who does.

896
00:54:06,833 --> 00:54:09,665
Swell. When you've told me,
that'll make two of us.

897
00:54:09,738 --> 00:54:14,130
Mathematically correct, sir, but I don't
know for certain that I'm going to tell you.

898
00:54:14,216 --> 00:54:17,269
Don't be foolish. I know where it is.
That's why I'm here.

899
00:54:17,343 --> 00:54:19,107
Well, sir, where is it?

900
00:54:19,518 --> 00:54:23,712
You see, I must tell you what I know,
but you won't tell me what you know.

901
00:54:23,884 --> 00:54:25,914
It's hardly equitable, sir.

902
00:54:26,321 --> 00:54:29,618
No, no. I don't think we can do business
along those lines.

903
00:54:29,691 --> 00:54:31,848
Well, think again, and think fast.

904
00:54:31,955 --> 00:54:34,552
I told that gunsel you'd
talk to me before you're through.

905
00:54:34,629 --> 00:54:37,114
You'll talk to me today,
or you are through.

906
00:54:37,189 --> 00:54:39,988
What are you wasting my time for?
I can get along without you.

907
00:54:40,100 --> 00:54:41,292
And

908
00:54:41,367 --> 00:54:43,698
keep that gunsel out of my way
while you're making up your mind.

909
00:54:43,762 --> 00:54:45,659
I'll kill him if you don't. I'll kill him.

910
00:54:45,739 --> 00:54:48,863
Well, sir, I must say
you have a most violent temper.

911
00:54:49,645 --> 00:54:52,232
SPADE:
Think it over. You've got till 5:00.

912
00:54:52,742 --> 00:54:55,504
Then you're either in or out, for keeps.

913
00:55:20,035 --> 00:55:22,421
-Who killed Thursby?
SPADE: I don't know.

914
00:55:22,539 --> 00:55:24,653
Perhaps, but you could make
an excellent guess.

915
00:55:24,730 --> 00:55:26,861
My guess might be excellent or crummy,

916
00:55:26,937 --> 00:55:33,787
but Mrs. Spade didn't raise any children...

917
00:55:30,174 --> 00:55:33,466
...dippy enough to make guesses in front
of a district attorney and a stenographer.

918
00:55:33,535 --> 00:55:35,269
Why, if you've nothing to conceal?

919
00:55:35,351 --> 00:55:37,235
Everybody has something to conceal.

920
00:55:37,344 --> 00:55:40,198
I'm a sworn officer of the law,
24 hours a day...

921
00:55:40,521 --> 00:55:43,813
...and neither formality nor informality
justifies you withholding...

922
00:55:43,882 --> 00:55:47,671
...evidence of crime from me except,
of course, on constitutional grounds.

923
00:55:47,750 --> 00:55:49,882
Both you and the police
have accused me...

924
00:55:49,960 --> 00:55:52,015
...of being mixed up in the murders.

925
00:55:52,091 --> 00:55:55,321
I've had trouble with both of you
before, and as far as I can see...

926
00:55:55,396 --> 00:55:58,313
...my best chance of clearing myself
of the trouble...

927
00:55:58,400 --> 00:56:00,494
...is bringing in the murderers
all tied up.

928
00:56:00,570 --> 00:56:01,992
The only chance I've got of tying them up

929
00:56:02,065 --> 00:56:03,801
and bringing them in...

930
00:56:03,868 --> 00:56:05,859
...is by staying away from you
and the police.

931
00:56:05,937 --> 00:56:07,266
You'd gum up the works.

932
00:56:07,377 --> 00:56:09,435
You getting
this all right? Am I going too fast?

933
00:56:09,540 --> 00:56:12,201
No, sir. I'm getting it all right.

934
00:56:12,607 --> 00:56:15,342
Good work. If you want to go to the board
and tell them I'm obstructing justice...

935
00:56:15,418 --> 00:56:17,564
...and ask them to revoke my license,
hop to it.

936
00:56:17,643 --> 00:56:20,447
You tried it before,
and it didn't get you anything.

937
00:56:20,549 --> 00:56:22,915
-Now, look here.
-I don't want any more informal talks.

938
00:56:22,984 --> 00:56:26,615
I've nothing to say to you,
and I'm tired of being called things...

939
00:56:26,685 --> 00:56:30,219
...by every crackpot on the city payroll.
So, if you want to see me...

940
00:56:29,929 --> 00:56:33,421
...pinch me or subpoena me or something,
and I'll come down with my lawyer.

941
00:56:33,496 --> 00:56:35,654
I'll see you at the inquest, maybe.

942
00:56:39,070 --> 00:56:40,859
Come on. He wants to see you.

943
00:56:40,938 --> 00:56:44,789
I didn't expect you till 5:25.
I hope I haven't kept you waiting.

944
00:56:45,205 --> 00:56:46,362
Keep on riding me,

945
00:56:46,442 --> 00:56:49,235
they're gonna be
picking iron out of your liver.

946
00:56:49,609 --> 00:56:52,461
[SPADE CHUCKLES]
The cheaper the crook,
the gaudier the patter, huh?

947
00:56:52,747 --> 00:56:54,229
Now, let's go.

948
00:57:15,228 --> 00:57:18,063
Come on.
This'll put you in solid with your boss.

949
00:57:26,711 --> 00:57:29,098
Come in, sir. Thank you for coming.
Come in.

950
00:57:29,177 --> 00:57:31,541
You shouldn't let him go around
with these on him.

951
00:57:31,645 --> 00:57:33,634
He might get himself hurt.

952
00:57:35,284 --> 00:57:36,611
Well, well, what's this?

953
00:57:36,680 --> 00:57:40,343
A crippled newsie took them away
from him. I made him give them back.

954
00:57:43,622 --> 00:57:48,250
By gad, sir, you're a chap worth knowing.
An amazing character. Give me your hat.

955
00:57:52,727 --> 00:57:54,026
Sit down.

956
00:57:59,868 --> 00:58:02,526
-I owe you an apology, sir.
SPADE: Now, never mind that.

957
00:58:02,871 --> 00:58:04,896
Let's talk about the black bird.

958
00:58:05,104 --> 00:58:06,696
All right, sir, let's.

959
00:58:09,845 --> 00:58:11,038
Let's.

960
00:58:11,983 --> 00:58:15,310
This is going to be the most astounding
thing you've ever heard of, sir.

961
00:58:15,380 --> 00:58:18,613
I say this knowing that a man
of your caliber and profession...

962
00:58:18,687 --> 00:58:21,706
...must have known
some astounding things in his time.

963
00:58:22,123 --> 00:58:25,750
What do you know about the Order
of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem...

964
00:58:25,822 --> 00:58:27,980
...later known as the Knights of Rhodes?

965
00:58:28,057 --> 00:58:31,449
-Crusaders or something, weren't they?
-Very good. Sit down.

966
00:58:34,297 --> 00:58:36,389
In 1539, these crusading knights...

967
00:58:36,505 --> 00:58:39,801
...persuaded Emperor Charles V
to give them the island of Malta.

968
00:58:39,873 --> 00:58:41,752
He made but one condition,

969
00:58:41,834 --> 00:58:44,327
that they pay
him each year the tribute of a falcon...

970
00:58:44,412 --> 00:58:47,069
...in acknowledgement that Malta
was still under Spain.

971
00:58:47,149 --> 00:58:48,730
-Do you follow me?
-Uh-huh.

972
00:58:50,718 --> 00:58:52,733
Have you any conception of the extreme,

973
00:58:52,809 --> 00:58:55,176
the immeasurable wealth of the Order?

974
00:58:55,250 --> 00:58:56,878
-I imagine they were pretty well fixed.
-"Pretty well" is putting it mildly.

975
00:58:56,947 --> 00:59:00,869
They were rolling in wealth, sir.

976
00:59:00,582 --> 00:59:04,136
For years they'd taken from the East
nobody knows what spoils of gems...

977
00:59:04,215 --> 00:59:06,508
...precious metals, silks, ivory, sir.

978
00:59:06,721 --> 00:59:10,651
We all know the holy wars to them
were largely a matter of loot.

979
00:59:11,231 --> 00:59:14,363
The knights were profoundly grateful
to the Emperor Charles...

980
00:59:14,437 --> 00:59:16,563
...for his generosity toward them.

981
00:59:16,637 --> 00:59:18,511
They hit upon the thought of sending him
for his first year's tribute...

982
00:59:18,593 --> 00:59:22,975
...not an insignificant live bird...

983
00:59:22,745 --> 00:59:24,858
...but a glorious golden falcon

984
00:59:25,073 --> 00:59:27,024
crusted from head to foot...

985
00:59:27,107 --> 00:59:29,631
...with the finest jewels in their coffers.

986
00:59:30,608 --> 00:59:31,727
-Well, sir,

987
00:59:31,808 --> 00:59:34,039
what do you think of that?
-I don't know.

988
00:59:34,109 --> 00:59:36,045
These are facts, historical facts.

989
00:59:36,127 --> 00:59:40,346
Not schoolbook history, not Mr. Wells'
history, but history, nevertheless.

990
00:59:40,486 --> 00:59:43,227
They sent this jeweled bird to Charles,
who was then in Spain.

991
00:59:43,295 --> 00:59:46,554
They sent it in a galley commanded
by a member of the Order.

992
00:59:47,625 --> 00:59:49,507
It never reached Spain.

993
00:59:50,030 --> 00:59:52,119
A famous admiral of buccaneers

994
00:59:52,571 --> 00:59:55,020
took the knights' galley and the bird.

995
00:59:55,558 --> 00:59:58,366
In 1713, it turned up in Sicily.

996
00:59:59,235 --> 01:00:01,758
In 1840, it appeared in Paris.

997
01:00:02,011 --> 01:00:05,096
It had, by that time,
acquired a coat of black enamel...

998
01:00:05,176 --> 01:00:09,277
...so that it looked nothing more
than a fairly interesting black statuette.

999
01:00:09,480 --> 01:00:13,174
In that disguise, sir, it was,
as you may say, kicked around Paris...

1000
01:00:13,678 --> 01:00:16,737
...for over three score years
by private owners too stupid...

1001
01:00:16,815 --> 01:00:18,738
...to see what it was under the skin.

1002
01:00:18,825 --> 01:00:19,982
Then, heh-heh,

1003
01:00:20,653 --> 01:00:23,282
in 1923,

1004
01:00:23,792 --> 01:00:27,873
a Greek dealer
named Charilaos Konstantinides
...found it in an obscure shop.

1005
01:00:28,895 --> 01:00:33,284
No thickness of enamel
could conceal value from his eyes.

1006
01:00:36,463 --> 01:00:39,762
-You begin to believe me a little?
-I haven't said I didn't.

1007
01:00:39,907 --> 01:00:44,658
Well, sir, to hold it safe while pursuing
his researches into its history...

1008
01:00:45,039 --> 01:00:47,202
...Charilaos re-enameled the bird.

1009
01:00:47,709 --> 01:00:51,474
Despite this precaution, however,
I got wind of his find.

1010
01:00:54,120 --> 01:00:56,109
If I'd only known a few days sooner.

1011
01:00:56,186 --> 01:00:57,743
I was in London

1012
01:00:57,822 --> 01:01:00,054
when I heard. I packed
a bag, got on the boat train immediately.

1013
01:01:00,127 --> 01:01:01,923
On the train I opened the paper,

1014
01:01:01,989 --> 01:01:03,519
and read that Charilaos' establishment...

1015
01:01:03,632 --> 01:01:06,044
...had been burglarized
and him murdered.

1016
01:01:06,258 --> 01:01:10,112
Sure enough, on arriving there,
I discovered the bird was gone.

1017
01:01:11,665 --> 01:01:13,388
That was 17 years ago.

1018
01:01:14,204 --> 01:01:18,426
Well, sir, it took me 17 years
to locate that bird, but I did.

1019
01:01:18,844 --> 01:01:20,105
I wanted it.

1020
01:01:20,340 --> 01:01:23,188
I'm a man not easily discouraged
when I want something.

1021
01:01:23,270 --> 01:01:25,365
I traced it to the home
of a Russian general, one Kemidov...

1022
01:01:25,444 --> 01:01:29,935
...in an Istanbul suburb.
He didn't know a thing about it.

1023
01:01:29,717 --> 01:01:31,880
It was nothing
but a black-enameled figure to him...

1024
01:01:31,958 --> 01:01:34,609
...but his contrariness kept him
from selling it...

1025
01:01:34,682 --> 01:01:38,570
...when I made him an offer.
So I sent him some agents to get it.

1026
01:01:38,882 --> 01:01:40,147
Well, sir,

1027
01:01:40,230 --> 01:01:42,745
they got it and I haven't got it.

1028
01:01:43,158 --> 01:01:45,523
But I'm going to get it.

1029
01:01:46,866 --> 01:01:48,188
Your glass.

1030
01:01:48,358 --> 01:01:52,398
Then the bird doesn't really belong
to any of you, but to a General Kemidov?

1031
01:01:52,473 --> 01:01:56,059
Well, sir, you might as well say
it belonged to the King of Spain.

1032
01:01:56,663 --> 01:02:00,150
I don't see how you can honestly grant
anyone else a clear title to it...

1033
01:02:00,235 --> 01:02:02,325
...except by right of possession.

1034
01:02:02,978 --> 01:02:04,274
And now, sir...

1035
01:02:04,706 --> 01:02:07,975
...before we start to talk prices,

1036
01:02:08,176 --> 01:02:10,601
how soon
can you, or how soon are you willing...

1037
01:02:10,677 --> 01:02:13,395
-...to produce the falcon?
-Couple of days.
That is satisfactory.

1038
01:02:14,089 --> 01:02:18,040
Well, sir, here's to a fair bargain.
Profits large enough for both of us.

1039
01:02:18,322 --> 01:02:20,374
What's your idea of a fair bargain?

1040
01:02:20,486 --> 01:02:24,045
I will give you $25,000
when you deliver the falcon to me...

1041
01:02:24,562 --> 01:02:26,278
...and another 25,000 later on.

1042
01:02:26,355 --> 01:02:29,782
Or I will give you one quarter
of what I realize on the falcon.

1043
01:02:29,899 --> 01:02:31,693
That would amount to a greater sum.

1044
01:02:31,769 --> 01:02:34,791
-How much greater?
-Who knows? Shall we say 100,000?

1045
01:02:35,071 --> 01:02:37,666
Would you believe me if I name a sum
that seems probable?

1046
01:02:37,744 --> 01:02:38,961
Why not?

1047
01:02:39,036 --> 01:02:41,634
What would you say
to a quarter of a million?

1048
01:02:43,881 --> 01:02:46,072
Then you think the dingus
is worth a million, huh?

1049
01:02:46,146 --> 01:02:47,971
In your own words, why not?

1050
01:02:52,116 --> 01:02:54,810
-That's a lot of dough.
-A lot of dough.

1051
01:02:57,089 --> 01:02:58,849
-Minimum, huh? What's the maximum?

1052
01:02:58,925 --> 01:03:01,252
-The maximum, I refuse to guess.

1053
01:03:02,357 --> 01:03:03,878
You'd think me crazy.

1054
01:03:04,128 --> 01:03:05,357
I don't know.

1055
01:03:06,064 --> 01:03:08,691
There's no telling
how high it could go, sir.

1056
01:03:08,899 --> 01:03:11,596
That is the one and only truth about it.

1057
01:03:28,847 --> 01:03:29,904
Wilmer.

1058
01:04:58,079 --> 01:04:59,703
Hello, Effie. It's me.

1059
01:05:00,050 --> 01:05:02,171
Let me talk to Miss O'Shaughnessy.

1060
01:05:07,256 --> 01:05:09,180
What? She didn't show up?

1061
01:05:11,556 --> 01:05:15,254
Oh.
You get on back to the office and
wait there till I come or you hear from me.

1062
01:05:15,332 --> 01:05:18,160
Yeah, let's do something right
for a change.

1063
01:06:10,810 --> 01:06:13,303
It started in the hold aft,
in the rear basement.

1064
01:06:13,382 --> 01:06:15,072
What insurance was she carrying?

1065
01:06:15,142 --> 01:06:16,299
-Anybody burnt?
-No.

1066
01:06:16,377 --> 01:06:18,473
Only the harbor watch was aboard.

1067
01:06:19,983 --> 01:06:21,642
-Someone I know came--
-Huh?

1068
01:06:21,716 --> 01:06:23,841
Someone I know came aboard
this afternoon...

1069
01:06:23,921 --> 01:06:26,149
...and I haven't seen her since.
I'm worried.

1070
01:06:26,226 --> 01:06:29,057
No reason to be, mister.
Everybody got off all right.

1071
01:06:29,123 --> 01:06:32,945
I wonder if you saw her.
She's kind of small, about 5-foot--

1072
01:06:33,028 --> 01:06:37,689
I couldn't tell you, mister. But if
she come aboard, she got off all right.

1073
01:06:37,764 --> 01:06:41,029
Only the harbor watch was aboard
when the fire started.

1074
01:06:43,097 --> 01:06:45,726
Now you know as much about it
as I do, precious.

1075
01:06:45,803 --> 01:06:48,363
Maybe they went down to the ship.
Maybe they didn't.

1076
01:06:48,441 --> 01:06:50,703
The part about the bird is thrilling.

1077
01:06:51,743 --> 01:06:53,141
Or ridiculous.

1078
01:07:04,658 --> 01:07:05,912
You know.

1079
01:07:08,693 --> 01:07:09,883
Falcon.

1080
01:07:16,268 --> 01:07:17,759
Lock that door.

1081
01:07:35,245 --> 01:07:36,335
Is...?

1082
01:07:37,015 --> 01:07:40,346
Is he...?
Yeah.

1083
01:07:41,417 --> 01:07:44,477
He couldn't have come far
with those holes in him.

1084
01:07:52,163 --> 01:07:55,822
Why couldn't he have stayed alive
long enough to tell us something?

1085
01:07:56,699 --> 01:08:00,031
Here, here, here, none of that now.
Come on.

1086
01:08:00,840 --> 01:08:03,893
-You can't pass out on me now.
-All right, Sam.

1087
01:08:12,749 --> 01:08:14,540
Do you really think it's...?

1088
01:08:28,493 --> 01:08:31,552
-We've got it, angel, we've got it.
-You're hurting me.

1089
01:08:33,295 --> 01:08:34,888
Answer the phone.

1090
01:08:37,800 --> 01:08:38,963
Hello.

1091
01:08:39,035 --> 01:08:40,125
[WOMAN SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY
OVER PHONE]

1092
01:08:40,635 --> 01:08:41,792
Yes. Who?

1093
01:08:43,872 --> 01:08:45,101
Oh, yes.

1094
01:08:45,873 --> 01:08:47,073
Where?

1095
01:08:47,804 --> 01:08:49,144
Yes, yes.

1096
01:08:50,850 --> 01:08:52,960
Hello? Hello. Hello.

1097
01:08:53,042 --> 01:08:55,702
That was Miss O'Shaughnessy.
She's in danger.

1098
01:08:55,782 --> 01:08:57,649
-Where is she?
-Burlingame, 26 Ancho.

1099
01:08:57,721 --> 01:08:59,208
Sam, her voice, it was awful.

1100
01:08:59,283 --> 01:09:02,273
Something happened to her
before she could finish. Go help her.

1101
01:09:02,352 --> 01:09:05,773
You've got to go to her.
He was helping her, and they killed him.

1102
01:09:05,860 --> 01:09:07,310
You've got to go, Sam.

1103
01:09:07,391 --> 01:09:08,949
All right, I'll go.

1104
01:09:09,425 --> 01:09:11,388
After I've gone,
you phone the police.

1105
01:09:11,463 --> 01:09:13,853
Tell them how it happened,
but don't drag names in.

1106
01:09:13,938 --> 01:09:16,856
I got the phone call and told you
I had to go, but didn't say where.

1107
01:09:16,935 --> 01:09:19,785
Forget this. Tell them how it happened,
without the bundle.

1108
01:09:19,873 --> 01:09:22,959
Get that straight. Everything
happened exactly as it did...

1109
01:09:23,039 --> 01:09:26,027
...but without the bundle,
and I got the phone call, not you.

1110
01:09:26,108 --> 01:09:28,099
-Yes, Sam.
-Okay.

1111
01:09:31,814 --> 01:09:35,043
Shut this door and lock it behind me.
Don't open it till the police come.

1112
01:09:35,116 --> 01:09:36,377
Do you know who he is?

1113
01:09:36,450 --> 01:09:39,684
Yeah. Yeah, he's Captain Jacoby,
master of the La Paloma.

1114
01:09:40,357 --> 01:09:42,447
You're a good man, sister.

1115
01:10:03,076 --> 01:10:05,103
-Can I borrow your pencil?
-Sure.

1116
01:10:31,033 --> 01:10:32,826
-Say, Frank.
-Oh, hello, Mr. Spade.

1117
01:10:32,899 --> 01:10:34,588
-You got plenty of gas?
-Sure thing.

1118
01:10:34,663 --> 01:10:36,789
Do you know where Ancho Street
is in Burlingame?

1119
01:10:36,868 --> 01:10:38,268
-Nope, but we can find her.

1120
01:10:38,338 --> 01:10:39,996
-Twenty-six is the number...

1121
01:10:40,070 --> 01:10:41,262
-...and the sooner the better.
-All right.

1122
01:10:50,678 --> 01:10:52,372
Keep your motor running.

1123
01:11:18,568 --> 01:11:20,690
-Bum steer, Mr. Spade?
-Yeah.

1124
01:11:27,152 --> 01:11:28,943
Let's get to a phone booth.

1125
01:11:29,651 --> 01:11:31,704
Hello, Mrs. Perine? Is Effie there?

1126
01:11:31,773 --> 01:11:33,116
Yes, please.

1127
01:11:34,447 --> 01:11:36,809
Hello, precious. What's the good news?

1128
01:11:37,221 --> 01:11:40,575
No, no. It was a bum steer.
Are you sure that was her voice?

1129
01:11:40,987 --> 01:11:42,584
Well, it was hooey.

1130
01:11:43,129 --> 01:11:46,420
Everything go all right?
Nothing said about the bundle, huh?

1131
01:11:46,666 --> 01:11:47,926
That's swell.

1132
01:11:47,996 --> 01:11:50,156
They take you down to the hall?

1133
01:11:51,298 --> 01:11:52,492
All right.

1134
01:11:52,574 --> 01:11:56,349
You better hit the hay and get
a good night's rest. You sound all in.

1135
01:11:56,439 --> 01:11:59,270
No, no. Save it till tomorrow.
I'm going on home.

1136
01:12:05,183 --> 01:12:08,199
-Thanks. Good night, Frank.
-Good night, Mr. Spade.

1137
01:12:15,522 --> 01:12:17,511
O'SHAUGHNESSY: I've been hiding
in a doorway up the street.

1138
01:12:17,593 --> 01:12:19,386
I thought you'd never come.

1139
01:12:29,096 --> 01:12:31,185
Can you make it,
or shall I carry you?

1140
01:12:31,266 --> 01:12:35,122
I'll be all right as soon as I can
get some place where I can lie down.

1141
01:12:48,414 --> 01:12:49,705
Well, sir, we're all here.

1142
01:12:49,778 --> 01:12:52,578
Now, let's come in and sit down
and be comfortable and talk.

1143
01:12:52,649 --> 01:12:53,836
SPADE:
Sure.

1144
01:12:54,555 --> 01:12:58,076
-Get away. You're not gonna frisk me.
-Stand still. Shut up.

1145
01:12:58,154 --> 01:13:00,743
Take your paws off me,
or I'll make you use that gun.

1146
01:13:00,825 --> 01:13:03,747
Ask your boss if he wants me shot up
before we talk.

1147
01:13:04,061 --> 01:13:05,520
Never mind, Wilmer.

1148
01:13:07,368 --> 01:13:10,058
You certainly are
a most headstrong individual.

1149
01:13:10,404 --> 01:13:12,260
Well, let's be seated.

1150
01:13:15,108 --> 01:13:18,695
Are you ready to make the first payment
and take the falcon off my hands?

1151
01:13:18,768 --> 01:13:20,967
Well, sir, as to that....

1152
01:13:21,947 --> 01:13:23,428
As to that....

1153
01:13:28,148 --> 01:13:31,140
Ten thousand? We were talking about
a lot more money than this.

1154
01:13:31,222 --> 01:13:34,277
Yes, sir, we were.
But this is genuine coin of the realm.

1155
01:13:34,355 --> 01:13:36,744
With a dollar of this,
you can buy $10 of talk.

1156
01:13:36,822 --> 01:13:39,210
And there are more of us
to be taken care of now.

1157
01:13:39,292 --> 01:13:41,445
That may be, but I've got the falcon.

1158
01:13:41,526 --> 01:13:44,115
I shouldn't think it necessary
to remind you, Mr. Spade...

1159
01:13:44,196 --> 01:13:47,112
...that you may have the falcon,
but we certainly have you.

1160
01:13:47,495 --> 01:13:50,689
I'm trying not to let that worry me.
We'll get back to the money later on.

1161
01:13:50,765 --> 01:13:53,462
There's something else
to be discussed first.

1162
01:13:55,541 --> 01:13:57,371
We've got to have a fall guy.

1163
01:13:57,442 --> 01:13:58,738
The police have gotta have a victim,

1164
01:13:58,812 --> 01:14:01,104
somebody they can pin those murders on.

1165
01:14:01,178 --> 01:14:04,770
Three? But there's only two, because
Thursby certainly killed your partner.

1166
01:14:04,841 --> 01:14:06,571
Only two, what difference does it make?

1167
01:14:06,646 --> 01:14:08,235
-We've got to give the police--
-Come, come, Mr. Spade.

1168
01:14:08,314 --> 01:14:12,447
You can't expect us to believe
at this late date...

1169
01:14:11,920 --> 01:14:13,939
...that you're the least bit afraid
of the police.

1170
01:14:14,018 --> 01:14:18,119
-Or that you're not able to handle--
-I'm in this up to my neck.

1171
01:14:17,319 --> 01:14:21,677
I've got to find somebody, a victim,
when the time comes. If I don't, I'll be it.

1172
01:14:26,970 --> 01:14:29,057
Let's give them the gunsel.

1173
01:14:29,442 --> 01:14:32,194
He actually did shoot Thursby and Jacoby,
didn't he?

1174
01:14:32,267 --> 01:14:35,032
Anyway, he's made to order for the part.
Look at him.

1175
01:14:35,109 --> 01:14:36,728
Let's give him to them.

1176
01:14:38,940 --> 01:14:41,635
By gad, sir, you are a character.
That you are.

1177
01:14:41,880 --> 01:14:44,536
There's never any telling
what you'll say or do next...

1178
01:14:44,614 --> 01:14:46,604
...except that it's bound
to be astonishing.

1179
01:14:46,677 --> 01:14:48,633
-It's our best bet. With him in their hands--

1180
01:14:48,719 --> 01:14:51,877
-My dear man, can't you see...

1181
01:14:51,955 --> 01:14:54,548
...that if I, even for a moment thought
of doing such a thing.... That's ridiculous.

1182
01:14:54,623 --> 01:14:58,244
I feel towards Wilmer here just exactly
as if he were my own son.

1183
01:14:58,319 --> 01:14:59,447
Really, I do.

1184
01:14:59,523 --> 01:15:02,440
But if I even for a moment
thought of doing what you propose...

1185
01:15:02,528 --> 01:15:05,861
...what in the world would keep Wilmer
from telling the police...

1186
01:15:05,930 --> 01:15:08,383
...every last detail
about the falcon and all--

1187
01:15:08,467 --> 01:15:09,859
Let him talk his head off.

1188
01:15:09,933 --> 01:15:13,065
I'll guarantee you
nobody'll do anything about it.

1189
01:15:13,373 --> 01:15:16,208
Well, well, what do you think of this,
Wilmer? Mighty funny.

1190
01:15:16,274 --> 01:15:17,728
Mighty funny.

1191
01:15:21,377 --> 01:15:23,831
How do you feel now?
Any better, precious?

1192
01:15:23,944 --> 01:15:25,273
[WHISPERING]
Much better.

1193
01:15:25,915 --> 01:15:27,308
But I'm frightened.

1194
01:15:27,386 --> 01:15:30,236
Well, don't be.
Nothing very bad's going to happen here.

1195
01:15:30,515 --> 01:15:32,506
You want a drink, angel?

1196
01:15:32,819 --> 01:15:34,445
Be careful, Sam.

1197
01:15:37,291 --> 01:15:38,377
Well?

1198
01:15:38,457 --> 01:15:40,122
If you're really serious,

1199
01:15:40,195 --> 01:15:42,922
the least we can do
in common politeness is hear you out.

1200
01:15:42,998 --> 01:15:45,515
Now, how would you be able to fix it

1201
01:15:45,900 --> 01:15:48,129
so that Wilmer couldn't do us any harm?

1202
01:15:48,200 --> 01:15:50,789
I can show our district attorney,
that if he goes around...

1203
01:15:50,870 --> 01:15:53,423
...trying to collect everybody,
he's gonna have a tangled case...

1204
01:15:53,502 --> 01:15:56,357
...but if he sticks to Wilmer here...

1205
01:15:56,573 --> 01:15:59,527
...he can get a conviction
standing on his head.

1206
01:16:02,787 --> 01:16:04,442
Get up on your feet.

1207
01:16:04,511 --> 01:16:07,468
I've taken all the riding from you
I'm gonna take.

1208
01:16:07,781 --> 01:16:09,311
Get up and shoot it out.

1209
01:16:09,389 --> 01:16:11,043
Young Wild West.

1210
01:16:11,221 --> 01:16:12,910
Tell him that shooting me
before you get your hands on the falcon...

1211
01:16:12,990 --> 01:16:16,153
...is gonna be bad for business.

1212
01:16:15,758 --> 01:16:18,444
Now, now, Wilmer. We can't have any
of that.

1213
01:16:18,523 --> 01:16:20,977
You shouldn't let yourself attach importance to these things.

1214
01:16:21,062 --> 01:16:22,584
-Then tell him to lay off me.

1215
01:16:22,665 --> 01:16:23,958
Now, Wilmer.

1216
01:16:26,331 --> 01:16:30,892
Your plan is not at all satisfactory, sir.
Let's not say anything more about it.

1217
01:16:30,972 --> 01:16:33,030
All right. I've got another suggestion.

1218
01:16:33,106 --> 01:16:36,157
It may not be as good as the first,
but it's better than nothing.

1219
01:16:36,242 --> 01:16:38,805
-You want to hear it?
-Most assuredly.

1220
01:16:42,911 --> 01:16:44,397
Give them Cairo.

1221
01:16:44,985 --> 01:16:46,240
Well, by gad, sir.

1222
01:16:46,315 --> 01:16:48,938
Suppose we give them you
or Miss O'Shaughnessy? How about that?

1223
01:16:49,013 --> 01:16:50,819
You want the falcon. I've got it.

1224
01:16:50,885 --> 01:16:54,550
The fall guy's part of the price
I'm asking.

1225
01:16:54,626 --> 01:16:59,155
As for Miss O'Shaughnessy, if you think
she can be rigged for the part...
...I'm willing
to discuss it with you.

1226
01:16:58,596 --> 01:17:02,383
You seem to forget that you are not
in a position at all to insist upon anything.

1227
01:17:02,465 --> 01:17:03,689
Now, come, gentlemen.

1228
01:17:03,769 --> 01:17:06,391
Let's keep
our discussion on a friendly basis.

1229
01:17:07,167 --> 01:17:10,220
There certainly is something
in what Mr. Cairo said.

1230
01:17:10,975 --> 01:17:14,325
If you kill me,
how are you gonna get the bird?

1231
01:17:14,407 --> 01:17:16,098
If I know you can't kill me,

1232
01:17:16,172 --> 01:17:18,235
how are
you going to scare me into giving it to you?

1233
01:17:18,311 --> 01:17:22,476
Sir, there are other means of persuasion
besides killing and threatening to kill.

1234
01:17:22,551 --> 01:17:23,980
SPADE:
Yes, that's....

1235
01:17:25,183 --> 01:17:26,618
That's true.

1236
01:17:28,218 --> 01:17:32,208
But they're none of them any good
unless the threat of death is behind them.

1237
01:17:32,288 --> 01:17:33,648
You see what I mean?

1238
01:17:33,726 --> 01:17:37,082
If you start something, I'll make it a matter
of your having to kill me,

1239
01:17:37,158 --> 01:17:38,611
or call it off.

1240
01:17:39,628 --> 01:17:43,856
That's an attitude, sir, that calls for
the most delicate judgment on both sides.

1241
01:17:43,933 --> 01:17:46,188
Because, as you know,
in the heat of action...

1242
01:17:46,267 --> 01:17:49,227
...men are likely to forget
where their best interests lie...

1243
01:17:49,307 --> 01:17:51,630
...and let their emotions
carry them away.

1244
01:17:51,703 --> 01:17:54,975
Then the trick from my angle is
to make my play strong enough...

1245
01:17:55,045 --> 01:17:57,964
...to tie you up, but not make you
mad enough to bump me off...

1246
01:17:58,046 --> 01:17:59,303
-...against your better judgment.

1247
01:17:59,382 --> 01:18:02,299
-By gad, sir, you are a character.

1248
01:18:07,655 --> 01:18:10,450
Six-two uneven.
They're selling you out, sonny.

1249
01:18:11,092 --> 01:18:13,515
I hope you're not letting yourself
be influenced by the guns...

1250
01:18:13,594 --> 01:18:16,417
...these pocket-edition desperadoes
are waving around.

1251
01:18:16,488 --> 01:18:19,691
Because I've practiced taking guns away
from these boys before...

1252
01:18:19,763 --> 01:18:22,096
...so we'll have no trouble there.

1253
01:18:22,367 --> 01:18:23,794
-Wilmer here is--
-All right.

1254
01:18:23,861 --> 01:18:26,319
Wilmer. Wilmer!

1255
01:18:27,229 --> 01:18:28,222
Wilmer!

1256
01:18:29,746 --> 01:18:31,414
Wilmer.

1257
01:18:57,439 --> 01:18:58,931
There's our fall guy.

1258
01:18:59,010 --> 01:19:02,200
Either you'll say yes, or I'll turn the falcon
and the whole lot of you in.

1259
01:19:02,274 --> 01:19:03,535
Don't like that, sir.

1260
01:19:03,612 --> 01:19:05,739
You won't like it. Well?

1261
01:19:10,557 --> 01:19:12,144
You can have him.

1262
01:19:13,722 --> 01:19:16,417
I won't be able to get the falcon
till daylight, or maybe later.

1263
01:19:16,497 --> 01:19:19,795
It strikes me it'd be best if we did not
get out of each other's sight...

1264
01:19:19,865 --> 01:19:23,255
...until our business has been transacted.
You have the envelope?

1265
01:19:23,335 --> 01:19:25,703
Miss O'Shaughnessy has it.
Hang on to it.

1266
01:19:25,768 --> 01:19:28,969
We don't have to lose sight of each other.
The dingus'll be brought here.

1267
01:19:29,046 --> 01:19:30,672
Excellent, sir, excellent.

1268
01:19:30,741 --> 01:19:33,072
And in exchange for the 10,000
and Wilmer...

1269
01:19:33,149 --> 01:19:35,773
...you'll give us the falcon
and an hour or two of grace.

1270
01:19:35,851 --> 01:19:38,212
Let's get the details fixed, first.

1271
01:19:38,287 --> 01:19:42,639
Why did he shoot Thursby and why, where
and how did he shoot Captain Jacoby?

1272
01:19:42,727 --> 01:19:44,779
I've got to know
all that happened, so I can be sure...

1273
01:19:44,860 --> 01:19:46,751
-...the parts that don't fit are covered up.

1274
01:19:46,833 --> 01:19:48,858
-I shall be candid with you, sir.

1275
01:19:48,932 --> 01:19:51,388
Thursby was Miss O'Shaughnessy's ally.

1276
01:19:51,471 --> 01:19:53,560
We believed that disposing of him...

1277
01:19:53,636 --> 01:19:57,564
...would cause Miss O'Shaughnessy to stop
and think that it would be best...

1278
01:19:57,638 --> 01:20:00,938
...to patch up her differences with us
regarding the falcon.

1279
01:20:01,175 --> 01:20:04,137
You didn't try to make a deal
with him before giving him the works?

1280
01:20:04,222 --> 01:20:06,408
We did. Yes, sir. We most certainly did.

1281
01:20:06,491 --> 01:20:09,981
I talked to him myself that very night,
but I could do nothing with him.

1282
01:20:10,058 --> 01:20:13,048
He was quite determinedly loyal
to Miss O'Shaughnessy.

1283
01:20:14,227 --> 01:20:17,581
So Wilmer followed him back to the hotel
and did what he did.

1284
01:20:18,168 --> 01:20:19,894
That sounds all right.

1285
01:20:20,700 --> 01:20:22,127
Now Jacoby.

1286
01:20:23,472 --> 01:20:27,368
Captain Jacoby's death was entirely
Miss O'Shaughnessy's fault.

1287
01:20:28,843 --> 01:20:30,148
SPADE:
Tell me what happened.

1288
01:20:30,215 --> 01:20:32,708
Cairo, as you must have surmised,
got in touch with me...

1289
01:20:32,784 --> 01:20:35,584
...after he left police headquarters
yesterday night.

1290
01:20:35,657 --> 01:20:38,918
He recognized the mutual advantage
of pooling forces.

1291
01:20:38,995 --> 01:20:41,554
Mr. Cairo is a man of nice judgment.

1292
01:20:41,697 --> 01:20:45,150
The Paloma was his thought. He saw
the notice of its arrival in the papers...

1293
01:20:45,227 --> 01:20:47,530
...and remembered he had heard
in Hong Kong...

1294
01:20:47,599 --> 01:20:50,833
...that Jacoby and Miss O'Shaughnessy
were seen together.

1295
01:20:53,813 --> 01:20:55,838
Well, sir, he saw that notice and,

1296
01:20:55,916 --> 01:20:58,474
putting
two and two together, guessed the truth.

1297
01:20:58,555 --> 01:21:01,214
She had given the bird to Jacoby
to bring here for her.

1298
01:21:01,292 --> 01:21:03,742
And at that juncture,
you decided to slip me a Mickey?

1299
01:21:03,826 --> 01:21:06,246
There was no place for you
in our plan, sir.

1300
01:21:06,325 --> 01:21:09,950
So we decided to spare ourselves
any possible embarrassment.

1301
01:21:10,827 --> 01:21:14,061
Mr. Cairo and Wilmer and I
went to call on Captain Jacoby.

1302
01:21:14,734 --> 01:21:18,087
We were lucky enough to arrive
while Miss O'Shaughnessy was there.

1303
01:21:18,167 --> 01:21:21,633
In many ways, the conference was
difficult, but we finally persuaded...

1304
01:21:21,714 --> 01:21:24,803
...Miss O'Shaughnessy to come to terms,
or so we thought.

1305
01:21:24,881 --> 01:21:27,336
We then left the boat
and set out for my hotel...

1306
01:21:27,419 --> 01:21:30,838
...where I was to pay Miss O'Shaughnessy
and receive the bird.

1307
01:21:30,990 --> 01:21:33,606
Well, sir, we mere men
should have known better.

1308
01:21:33,687 --> 01:21:36,248
En route, she, Captain Jacoby
and the falcon...

1309
01:21:36,327 --> 01:21:38,950
...slipped completely through our fingers.

1310
01:21:39,164 --> 01:21:41,616
It was neatly done, sir. Indeed, it was.

1311
01:21:42,502 --> 01:21:44,665
You touched off the boat before you left?

1312
01:21:44,739 --> 01:21:46,195
No, not intentionally.

1313
01:21:46,275 --> 01:21:50,008
Though, I daresay, we, or Wilmer at least,
were responsible for the fire.

1314
01:21:50,081 --> 01:21:54,607
While the rest of us were talking
in the cabin, Wilmer went about the boat...

1315
01:21:55,121 --> 01:21:57,008
...trying to find the falcon.

1316
01:21:57,953 --> 01:22:00,178
No doubt, he was careless with matches.

1317
01:22:00,251 --> 01:22:01,810
How about the shooting?

1318
01:22:01,888 --> 01:22:05,419
We caught up with Miss O'Shaughnessy
and Jacoby at her apartment.

1319
01:22:05,497 --> 01:22:09,487
I sent Wilmer downstairs to cover
the fire escape before ringing the bell.

1320
01:22:09,932 --> 01:22:13,500
And, sure enough, while she was asking us
who we were through the door...

1321
01:22:13,579 --> 01:22:16,270
...and we were telling her,
we heard a window go up.

1322
01:22:16,342 --> 01:22:19,366
Wilmer shot Jacoby
as he was coming down the fire escape...

1323
01:22:19,447 --> 01:22:23,442
...shot him more than once, but Jacoby
was too tough to fall or drop the falcon.

1324
01:22:23,514 --> 01:22:27,336
He climbed down the rest of the way,
knocked Wilmer over and ran off.

1325
01:22:28,789 --> 01:22:32,521
We persuaded-- That is the word, sir.
We persuaded Miss O'Shaughnessy...

1326
01:22:32,592 --> 01:22:35,657
...to tell us where she had told
Captain Jacoby to take the falcon.

1327
01:22:35,733 --> 01:22:38,189
And we further persuaded her
to phone your office...

1328
01:22:38,271 --> 01:22:41,399
...in an attempt to draw you away
before Jacoby got there...

1329
01:22:41,477 --> 01:22:43,062
...but, unfortunately for us...

1330
01:22:43,143 --> 01:22:46,407
...it had taken us too long
to persuade Miss O'Shaughnessy.

1331
01:22:46,779 --> 01:22:48,836
And you had the falcon
before we could reach you.

1332
01:23:04,168 --> 01:23:07,060
Wilmer, I'm sorry indeed to lose you,
but I want you to know...

1333
01:23:07,135 --> 01:23:09,869
...I couldn't be fonder of you
if you were my own son.

1334
01:23:09,935 --> 01:23:12,964
But, well, if you lose a son,
it's possible to get another.

1335
01:23:13,534 --> 01:23:15,699
There's only one Maltese Falcon.

1336
01:23:16,981 --> 01:23:20,278
When you're young,
you simply don't understand these things.

1337
01:23:22,325 --> 01:23:24,914
How about some coffee?
Put the pot on, will you, angel?

1338
01:23:24,988 --> 01:23:27,087
I don't like to leave our guests.

1339
01:23:27,201 --> 01:23:28,326
O'SHAUGHNESSY: Surely.
-Just a moment, my dear.

1340
01:23:28,398 --> 01:23:32,339
Hadn't you better
leave the envelope in here?

1341
01:23:33,966 --> 01:23:35,770
Sit on it, if you're afraid of losing it.

1342
01:23:35,840 --> 01:23:37,793
You misunderstand me.
It's not that at all.

1343
01:23:37,877 --> 01:23:41,330
But business should be transacted
in a businesslike manner.

1344
01:23:43,134 --> 01:23:46,173
For instance,
there are only nine bills here now.

1345
01:23:46,252 --> 01:23:49,779
There were 10 when I handed them to you,
as you very well know.

1346
01:23:56,494 --> 01:23:57,649
Well?

1347
01:23:57,765 --> 01:23:59,602
I want to know about this.

1348
01:24:07,335 --> 01:24:08,705
You palmed it.

1349
01:24:08,815 --> 01:24:09,898
-I palmed it?
-Yes.

1350
01:24:09,978 --> 01:24:12,031
Do you want to stand for a frisk?

1351
01:24:12,114 --> 01:24:15,268
You're going to admit it, or I'm gonna
search you. There's no third way.

1352
01:24:15,346 --> 01:24:17,121
I believe you would. I do.

1353
01:24:17,196 --> 01:24:19,209
You are
a character, if you don't mind my saying so.

1354
01:24:19,291 --> 01:24:20,752
You palmed it.

1355
01:24:21,323 --> 01:24:23,196
Yes, sir, that I did.

1356
01:24:27,929 --> 01:24:30,230
I must have my little joke now and then.

1357
01:24:30,302 --> 01:24:33,353
I was curious to know what
you would do in a situation of this sort.

1358
01:24:33,433 --> 01:24:35,799
I must say you passed the test
with flying colors.

1359
01:24:35,871 --> 01:24:38,295
It's the sort of thing I'd expect
from somebody Wilmer's age.

1360
01:24:38,376 --> 01:24:41,145
This will soon be yours.
You might as well take it.

1361
01:24:41,215 --> 01:24:42,904
I ought to have more than 10,000.

1362
01:24:42,984 --> 01:24:45,372
You understand
this is the first payment. Later.

1363
01:24:45,456 --> 01:24:48,118
Oh, yes. Later you'll give me millions,

1364
01:24:48,194 --> 01:24:50,080
but how's about 15,000 now?

1365
01:24:50,396 --> 01:24:53,746
Frankly and candidly,
upon my word of honor as a gentleman...

1366
01:24:54,262 --> 01:24:56,860
... 10,000 is all the money I can raise.

1367
01:24:58,100 --> 01:25:00,029
But you didn't say positively.

1368
01:25:00,439 --> 01:25:01,731
Positively.

1369
01:25:07,013 --> 01:25:09,308
I'd like to give you a word of advice.

1370
01:25:09,381 --> 01:25:10,406
-Go ahead.

1371
01:25:11,991 --> 01:25:13,549
-I daresay you're going to give her money...

1372
01:25:13,625 --> 01:25:16,086
...but if you don't give her as much
as she thinks she ought to have...

1373
01:25:16,160 --> 01:25:18,612
...my word of advice is be careful.

1374
01:25:22,027 --> 01:25:23,328
Dangerous?

1375
01:25:24,033 --> 01:25:25,054
Very.

1376
01:25:26,300 --> 01:25:29,261
-How's the coffee coming, angel?
O'SHAUGHNESSY: In a few minutes.

1377
01:25:29,338 --> 01:25:31,634
It's almost daylight, Mr. Spade.

1378
01:25:31,975 --> 01:25:34,036
Can you start getting it now?

1379
01:25:34,309 --> 01:25:35,683
I guess so.

1380
01:25:48,796 --> 01:25:49,915
Hello?

1381
01:25:50,392 --> 01:25:53,254
Hello, precious.
I'm sorry to get you up so early.

1382
01:25:53,703 --> 01:25:56,001
Now, listen carefully. Here's the plot.

1383
01:25:56,069 --> 01:26:00,063
In the holland box at the post office
there's an envelope with my scrawl.

1384
01:26:00,508 --> 01:26:04,870
In that envelope, there's a parcel-room
check for the bundle we got yesterday.

1385
01:26:05,655 --> 01:26:08,645
Uh-huh.
Now, get that bundle
and bring it here, pdq.

1386
01:26:08,716 --> 01:26:11,886
That's a good girl. Now hustle. Goodbye.

1387
01:27:01,283 --> 01:27:03,541
Thanks, lady.
Sorry to spoil your day of rest.

1388
01:27:03,619 --> 01:27:05,776
Not the first one you've spoiled.
Anything else?

1389
01:27:05,852 --> 01:27:08,181
-No. No, thanks.
-Bye-bye, then.

1390
01:27:27,982 --> 01:27:29,502
SPADE:
There you are.

1391
01:27:41,259 --> 01:27:43,686
Now, after 17 years....

1392
01:28:22,577 --> 01:28:23,832
It is it.

1393
01:28:25,283 --> 01:28:27,019
But we'll make sure.

1394
01:28:44,610 --> 01:28:45,741
Fake.

1395
01:28:45,810 --> 01:28:47,838
It's a phony. It's lead.

1396
01:28:48,346 --> 01:28:49,605
It's lead.

1397
01:28:50,012 --> 01:28:51,379
It's a fake.

1398
01:28:52,375 --> 01:28:54,800
You've had your little joke.
Now, tell us about it.

1399
01:28:54,882 --> 01:28:56,288
No, Sam. No.

1400
01:28:56,730 --> 01:28:59,026
That's the one I got from Kemidov.
I swear it.

1401
01:28:59,097 --> 01:29:00,218
You.

1402
01:29:01,533 --> 01:29:03,591
It's you who bungled it.

1403
01:29:04,231 --> 01:29:07,032
You and your stupid attempt to buy it.

1404
01:29:07,361 --> 01:29:10,087
Kemidov found out how valuable it was.

1405
01:29:10,540 --> 01:29:12,831
No wonder we had
such an easy time stealing it.

1406
01:29:12,906 --> 01:29:14,408
You imbecile.

1407
01:29:14,485 --> 01:29:16,711
You bloated idiot.

1408
01:29:16,789 --> 01:29:18,671
You stupid fathead, you.

1409
01:29:47,216 --> 01:29:50,246
Yes, it's the Russian's hand.
There's no doubt about it.

1410
01:29:50,322 --> 01:29:51,784
Well, sir, what do you suggest?

1411
01:29:51,858 --> 01:29:54,448
We shed tears
and call each other names...

1412
01:29:54,526 --> 01:29:56,621
...or shall we go to Istanbul?

1413
01:29:58,056 --> 01:29:59,687
Are you going?

1414
01:29:59,897 --> 01:30:03,855
Seventeen years I've wanted that little
item and have been trying to get it.

1415
01:30:03,939 --> 01:30:06,525
If we must spend another year
on the quest...

1416
01:30:06,605 --> 01:30:09,906
...well, sir, it will be an additional
expenditure in time...

1417
01:30:09,975 --> 01:30:13,671
...of only five
and fifteen-seventeenths percent.

1418
01:30:14,253 --> 01:30:15,938
I'm going with you.

1419
01:30:32,373 --> 01:30:33,588
Wilmer.

1420
01:30:36,301 --> 01:30:37,938
[SPADE LAUGHS]
A swell lot of thieves.

1421
01:30:38,017 --> 01:30:41,172
GUTMAN: We've little to boast about,
but the world hasn't come to an end...

1422
01:30:41,241 --> 01:30:43,236
...just because we've run into a setback.

1423
01:30:43,312 --> 01:30:46,699
-I must ask you for that envelope.
-I held up my end. You got your dingus.

1424
01:30:46,790 --> 01:30:49,077
It's your hard luck
it wasn't what you wanted.

1425
01:30:49,150 --> 01:30:51,147
Now, come, sir, we've all failed...

1426
01:30:51,223 --> 01:30:55,227
...and there's no reason for expecting
any of us to bear the whole brunt.

1427
01:30:55,542 --> 01:30:58,265
In short, sir,
I must ask you for my 10,000.

1428
01:31:02,606 --> 01:31:05,099
This'll take care
of my time and expenses.

1429
01:31:08,155 --> 01:31:10,102
Now, sir, we'll say goodbye to you...

1430
01:31:10,179 --> 01:31:13,647
...unless you care to undertake
the Istanbul expedition with us.

1431
01:31:13,726 --> 01:31:14,974
You don't?

1432
01:31:15,153 --> 01:31:17,535
Well, frankly, sir,
I'd like to have you along.

1433
01:31:17,624 --> 01:31:20,255
You're a man of nice judgment
and many resources.

1434
01:31:20,328 --> 01:31:21,786
Now that there's no alternative...

1435
01:31:21,858 --> 01:31:23,834
...I daresay you'll manage
without a fall guy.

1436
01:31:23,910 --> 01:31:25,090
-I'll make out all right.
-Well, sir, the shortest farewells are best.

1437
01:31:25,166 --> 01:31:29,274
Adieu.

1438
01:31:32,305 --> 01:31:36,095
And to you, Miss O'Shaughnessy,
I leave the rara avis on the table there...

1439
01:31:36,185 --> 01:31:37,802
...as a little memento.

1440
01:31:57,965 --> 01:31:59,956
Hello. Is Sergeant Polhaus there?

1441
01:32:00,202 --> 01:32:02,501
Yeah. Put him on. This is Sam Spade.

1442
01:32:03,882 --> 01:32:07,114
Hello. Tom? Now, listen.
I've got something for you. Here it is.

1443
01:32:07,190 --> 01:32:10,274
Jacoby and Thursby were killed
by a kid named Wilmer Cook.

1444
01:32:10,353 --> 01:32:13,918
Yeah. He's about 20 years old, 5'6",
wearing a gray overcoat.

1445
01:32:14,064 --> 01:32:16,255
He's working for a man
named Kasper Gutman.

1446
01:32:16,329 --> 01:32:18,991
Now, you can't miss Gutman.
He must weigh 300 pounds.

1447
01:32:19,070 --> 01:32:20,926
That fellow Cairo's in with him too.

1448
01:32:20,998 --> 01:32:22,788
They just left
for the Alexandria Hotel.

1449
01:32:22,856 --> 01:32:25,202
You'll have to move fast.
They're blowing town.

1450
01:32:25,274 --> 01:32:27,401
Watch yourself when you go up
against the kid.

1451
01:32:27,476 --> 01:32:29,203
Yes, that's right. Very.

1452
01:32:29,280 --> 01:32:30,860
Now, good luck, Tom.

1453
01:32:30,939 --> 01:32:33,462
They'll talk, when they're nailed.
We're sitting on dynamite.

1454
01:32:33,549 --> 01:32:36,680
We've only got minutes to get set.
Give me all of it.

1455
01:32:36,760 --> 01:32:39,319
When you came to my office,
why did you want Thursby shadowed?

1456
01:32:39,395 --> 01:32:41,918
I told you, Sam. I thought he was
betraying me.

1457
01:32:41,999 --> 01:32:43,273
You had Thursby hooked.

1458
01:32:43,343 --> 01:32:46,416
You wanted
to get rid of him before Jacoby came...

1459
01:32:46,500 --> 01:32:49,268
...with the loot so you wouldn't have to
split it with him. Isn't that so?

1460
01:32:49,346 --> 01:32:51,013
I thought if he knew someone was following
him

1461
01:32:51,084 --> 01:32:52,404
he'd be frightened into going away.

1462
01:32:52,476 --> 01:32:55,175
Miles wasn't clumsy enough
to be spotted the first night.

1463
01:32:55,252 --> 01:32:57,704
You told Thursby he was being followed.

1464
01:33:00,124 --> 01:33:01,469
I told him.

1465
01:33:02,694 --> 01:33:04,043
I told him.

1466
01:33:04,938 --> 01:33:06,239
Yes, but please believe me, Sam.

1467
01:33:06,306 --> 01:33:08,674
I wouldn't have
if I thought Floyd would kill him.

1468
01:33:08,745 --> 01:33:11,200
If you thought he wouldn't kill Miles,
you were right.

1469
01:33:11,280 --> 01:33:13,668
Miles hadn't many brains, but he'd had
too many years as a detective...

1470
01:33:13,752 --> 01:33:18,925
...to be caught by a man he was shadowing

1471
01:33:16,257 --> 01:33:19,246
up a blind alley with his gun on his hip.

1472
01:33:19,324 --> 01:33:21,749
But he'd have gone up there
with you, angel.

1473
01:33:21,828 --> 01:33:24,092
He was just dumb enough for that.

1474
01:33:24,432 --> 01:33:27,362
He'd have looked you up and down, licked
his lips

1475
01:33:27,448 --> 01:33:29,268
and gone grinning from ear to ear.

1476
01:33:29,401 --> 01:33:31,793
You could have stood
as close to him as you liked...

1477
01:33:31,876 --> 01:33:34,427
...and put a hole through him with a gun
you got from Thursby.

1478
01:33:34,509 --> 01:33:35,513
-Don't, Sam.

1479
01:33:35,595 --> 01:33:37,308
-The police'll be here any minute, now talk.

1480
01:33:37,386 --> 01:33:38,536
-Why do you accuse me?

1481
01:33:38,615 --> 01:33:40,350
-This isn't the time for that act.

1482
01:33:40,425 --> 01:33:41,998
We're both of us sitting under
the gallows.

1483
01:33:42,079 --> 01:33:44,254
Now, why did you shoot Miles?

1484
01:33:45,333 --> 01:33:48,335
I didn't mean to at first, really, I didn't.

1485
01:33:49,368 --> 01:33:52,247
But when I found out
that Floyd couldn't be frightened....

1486
01:33:52,332 --> 01:33:54,376
I can't look at you and tell you this, Sam.

1487
01:33:54,442 --> 01:33:57,455
You thought Thursby would tackle
Miles and one would go down.

1488
01:33:57,539 --> 01:33:59,097
If Thursby was killed,
you were rid of him.

1489
01:33:59,170 --> 01:34:01,393
If it was Miles,
you'd see Thursby set up for it.

1490
01:34:01,472 --> 01:34:02,889
-Something like that.

1491
01:34:02,973 --> 01:34:04,681
-When Thursby wasn't going to...

1492
01:34:04,750 --> 01:34:07,001
...you borrowed his gun
and did it yourself.

1493
01:34:07,078 --> 01:34:09,335
When you heard Thursby was shot,
you knew Gutman was in town.

1494
01:34:09,410 --> 01:34:10,988
You needed somebody

1495
01:34:11,063 --> 01:34:14,436
to fill
Thursby's boots, so you came back to me.

1496
01:34:14,519 --> 01:34:17,564
Yes. Oh, sweetheart, it wasn't only that.

1497
01:34:18,058 --> 01:34:20,293
I'd have come back to you,
sooner or later.

1498
01:34:20,365 --> 01:34:23,192
From the very first instant I saw you,
I knew.

1499
01:34:25,126 --> 01:34:29,686
Well, if you get a good break,
you'll be out of Tehachapi in 20 years...

1500
01:34:29,764 --> 01:34:31,955
...and you can come back to me then.

1501
01:34:32,367 --> 01:34:35,799
I hope they don't hang you, precious,
by that sweet neck.

1502
01:34:38,263 --> 01:34:41,173
-You're not....
-Yes, angel, I'm gonna send you over.

1503
01:34:41,261 --> 01:34:43,619
The chances are you'll get off with life.

1504
01:34:43,696 --> 01:34:46,250
That means, if you're a good girl, you'll
be out in 20 years.

1505
01:34:46,327 --> 01:34:48,047
I'll be waiting for you.

1506
01:34:48,119 --> 01:34:50,711
If they hang you,
I'll always remember you.

1507
01:34:53,385 --> 01:34:54,744
Don't, Sam.

1508
01:34:55,225 --> 01:34:57,244
Don't say that, even in fun.

1509
01:34:58,730 --> 01:35:01,978
I was frightened for a minute.
I really thought....

1510
01:35:02,496 --> 01:35:04,950
You do such wild
and unpredictable things.

1511
01:35:05,030 --> 01:35:06,746
Now, don't be silly.

1512
01:35:06,855 --> 01:35:08,739
You're taking the fall.

1513
01:35:14,665 --> 01:35:16,606
You've been playing with me.

1514
01:35:17,134 --> 01:35:19,900
Just pretending you cared
to trap me like this.

1515
01:35:19,970 --> 01:35:21,848
You didn't care at all.

1516
01:35:22,611 --> 01:35:24,117
You don't love me.

1517
01:35:24,194 --> 01:35:26,053
I won't play the sap for you.

1518
01:35:26,161 --> 01:35:27,702
It's not like that. You can't say that.

1519
01:35:27,781 --> 01:35:29,818
You never played square with me

1520
01:35:29,890 --> 01:35:30,885
since I've known you.

1521
01:35:30,995 --> 01:35:34,534
You know deep in your heart that
in spite of anything I've done, I love you.

1522
01:35:34,608 --> 01:35:36,701
I don't care who loves who.
I won't play the sap.

1523
01:35:36,771 --> 01:35:40,222
I won't walk in Thursby's and I don't know
how many others' footsteps.

1524
01:35:40,299 --> 01:35:43,290
You killed Miles,
and you're going over for it.

1525
01:35:56,187 --> 01:35:58,445
How can you do this to me, Sam?

1526
01:35:59,734 --> 01:36:02,695
Surely Mr. Archer wasn't
as much to you as....

1527
01:36:15,455 --> 01:36:16,674
Listen.

1528
01:36:17,515 --> 01:36:19,474
This won't do any good.

1529
01:36:20,259 --> 01:36:23,714
You'll never understand me,
but I'll try once and then give it up.

1530
01:36:23,795 --> 01:36:27,052
When a man's partner is killed,
he's supposed to do something.

1531
01:36:27,126 --> 01:36:29,358
Doesn't make any difference
what you thought of him.

1532
01:36:29,430 --> 01:36:32,954
He was your partner, and you're supposed
to do something about it.

1533
01:36:33,404 --> 01:36:36,236
And it happens
we're in the detective business.

1534
01:36:36,342 --> 01:36:39,466
Well, when one of your organization
gets killed, it's....

1535
01:36:39,835 --> 01:36:42,966
It's bad business
to let the killer get away with it.

1536
01:36:43,043 --> 01:36:45,677
Bad all around,
bad for every detective everywhere.

1537
01:36:45,751 --> 01:36:48,080
You don't expect me to think
these things...

1538
01:36:48,153 --> 01:36:49,613
-...are sufficient reason for sending me--

1539
01:36:49,691 --> 01:36:52,214
-Wait till I'm through. Then you can talk.

1540
01:36:52,295 --> 01:36:54,487
I've no earthly reason to think
I can trust you.

1541
01:36:54,564 --> 01:36:57,553
If I do this and get away with it,
you'll have something on me...

1542
01:36:57,633 --> 01:36:59,859
...that you can use
whenever you want to.

1543
01:36:59,933 --> 01:37:01,428
Since I've got something on you...

1544
01:37:01,510 --> 01:37:05,065
...I couldn't be sure that you wouldn't
put a hole in me someday.

1545
01:37:05,669 --> 01:37:07,596
All those are on one side.

1546
01:37:07,849 --> 01:37:11,406
Maybe some of them are unimportant.
I won't argue about that.

1547
01:37:12,017 --> 01:37:14,212
But look at the number of them.

1548
01:37:15,121 --> 01:37:16,955
And what have we got on the other side?

1549
01:37:17,023 --> 01:37:21,252
All we've got is that maybe you love me
and maybe I love you.

1550
01:37:22,466 --> 01:37:24,845
You know whether you love me or not.

1551
01:37:26,597 --> 01:37:31,160
Maybe I do. I'll have some rotten nights
after I've sent you over, but that'll pass.

1552
01:37:36,176 --> 01:37:38,377
If all I've said doesn't mean anything to you

1553
01:37:38,444 --> 01:37:41,077
then forget it and we'll make it just this.

1554
01:37:41,156 --> 01:37:44,709
I won't because all of me wants to
regardless of consequences.

1555
01:37:44,951 --> 01:37:46,652
You counted on that with me the same

1556
01:37:46,725 --> 01:37:49,211
as you counted on that with all the others.

1557
01:37:49,298 --> 01:37:52,715
Would you have done this if the falcon
had been real and you'd got your money?

1558
01:37:52,799 --> 01:37:55,465
Don't be too sure I'm as crooked
as I'm supposed to be.

1559
01:37:55,534 --> 01:37:57,956
That sort of reputation
might be good business...

1560
01:37:58,039 --> 01:38:02,099
...bringing high-priced jobs and making it
easier to deal with the enemy...

1561
01:38:02,545 --> 01:38:07,365
...but a lot more money would have been
one more item on your side of the scales.

1562
01:38:07,452 --> 01:38:11,379
If you'd loved me, you wouldn't have
needed any more on that side.

1563
01:38:23,071 --> 01:38:24,264
Come in.

1564
01:38:29,014 --> 01:38:30,895
Hello, Tom. Got them?

1565
01:38:30,977 --> 01:38:32,069
POLHAUS: Got them.
-Swell.

1566
01:38:32,146 --> 01:38:36,409
Here's another one for you.
She killed Miles.

1567
01:38:36,357 --> 01:38:39,842
Oh, and I've got some exhibits.
The boys' guns, one of Cairo's...

1568
01:38:39,918 --> 01:38:42,753
...and a $1,000 bill I was supposed
to be bribed with.

1569
01:38:43,056 --> 01:38:46,415
And this black statuette here
that all the fuss was about.

1570
01:38:47,193 --> 01:38:48,660
What's the matter with your playmate?

1571
01:38:48,742 --> 01:38:50,124
He looks brokenhearted.

1572
01:38:50,198 --> 01:38:52,688
I bet when he heard Gutman's story,
he thought he had me.

1573
01:38:52,769 --> 01:38:54,333
Cut it out, Sam.

1574
01:38:55,572 --> 01:38:58,202
Well, shall we be getting down
to the hall?

1575
01:39:09,559 --> 01:39:10,917
It's heavy.

1576
01:39:11,326 --> 01:39:12,652
What is it?

1577
01:39:17,000 --> 01:39:20,552
The stuff that dreams are made of.

